window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-03-1", "source": "2023年03月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “People are now increasingly aware of the danger of ‘appearance anxiety’ or being obsessed with one’s looks.” You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "People are now increasingly aware of the danger of ‘appearance anxiety’ or being obsessed with one’s looks.\nThis uneasiness rarely appears out of nowhere. Bombarded daily by filtered images and narrow aesthetic\nstandards on social media, many young people measure themselves against unreachable models and feel that\nthey never look good enough. The constant comparison quietly drains their confidence and even distracts them\nfrom things that truly matter.\nYet a healthier attitude is well within our reach. The key is to shift attention from the mirror to inner value —\nour knowledge, kindness and abilities. I once spent hours worrying about a small scar before a presentation,\nuntil a friend reminded me that the audience remembered my ideas, not my face. That moment taught me that\nself-acceptance, rather than a flawless appearance, is what sets us free.\nIn short, looks are only a thin surface of who we are. Once we learn to value character over appearance,\n‘appearance anxiety’ loses its grip, and we can live with genuine confidence."}, "translation": {"prompt": "张骞(Zhang Qian)是中国第一个伟大的探险家。他不畏艰险，克服重重困难，两次出使西域，开通了中国同西亚和欧洲的通商关系，将中国的丝和丝织品运往西亚和欧洲，开拓了历史上著名的\"丝绸之路\"。同时，他又将西域的风土人情、地理文化以及特有物种等介绍到中原，极大地开阔了人们的视野。 正如历史学家所指出的那样，如果没有张骞出使西域，就不可能有丝绸之路的开辟，也就不会有汉朝同西域或欧洲的文化交流。", "sample": "Zhang Qian was the first great explorer in Chinese history. Braving dangers and overcoming numerous difficulties, he made two journeys to the Western Regions, opened up trade between China, West Asia and Europe, transported Chinese silk and silk fabrics there, and thereby pioneered the famous Silk Road in history. Meanwhile, he introduced the local customs, geography, culture and unique species of the Western Regions to the Central Plains, greatly broadening people's horizons. As historians have pointed out, without Zhang Qian's mission to the Western Regions, there would have been no opening of the Silk Road, nor any cultural exchanges between the Han Dynasty and the Western Regions or Europe."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2023-03-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Unthinkable as it may be, humanity, every last person, could someday be wiped from the face of the Earth. We have learned to worry about asteroids(小行星) and super volcanoes, but the more likely  __26__  , according to Nick Bostrom, a professor of philosophy at Oxford, is that we humans will destroy ourselves.\nProfessor Bostrom, who directs Oxford's Future of Humanity Institute, has argued over the course of several papers that human  __27__  risks are poorly understood and, worse still,  __28__  underestimated by society. Some of these existential risks are fairly well known, especially the natural ones. But others are  __29__  or even exotic. Most worrying to Bostrom is the subset of existential risks that  __30__  from human technology, a subset that he expects to grow in number and potency over the next century.\nDespite his concerns about the risks  __31__  to humans by technological progress, Bostrom is no luddite(科技进步反对者). In fact, he is a longtime  __32__  of trans-humanism—the effort to improve the human condition, and even human nature itself, through technological means. In the long run he sees technology as a bridge, a bridge we humans must cross with great care, in order to reach new and better modes of being. In his work, Bostrom uses the tools of philosophy and mathematics, in  __33__  , probability theory, to try and determine how we as a  __34__  might achieve this safe passage. What follows is my conversation with Bostrom about some of the most interesting and worrying existential risks that humanity might  __35__  in the decades and centuries to come, and about what we can do to make sure we outlast them.", "matchIntro": "San Francisco Has Become One Huge Metaphor for Economic Inequality in America", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The fog still chills the morning air and the cable cars still climb halfway to the stars. Yet on the ground, the Bay area has changed greatly since singer Tony Bennett left his heart here. Silicon Valley and the tech industry have led the region into a period of unprecedented wealth and innovation. But existing political and land limits have caused an alarming housing crisis and astronomical rise in social and economic difference."}, {"label": "B", "text": "While the residents of most cities display pride and support for their home industries, drastic market distortions in the San Francisco Bay Area have created boiling resentment in the region towards the tech industry. A vocal minority is even calling on officials to punish those who are benefitting from the economic and housing boom. If this boom and its consequences are not resolved, a drastic increase in social and economic difference may have a profound impact on the region for generations. A history and analysis of this transformation may hold invaluable insights about the opportunities. Perils of tech cities are currently being cultivated across the US, and indeed around the world."}, {"label": "C", "text": "According to a recent study, San Francisco ranks first in California for economic difference. The average income of the top 1% of households in the city averages $3.6 million. This is 44 times the average income of those at the bottom, which stands at $81,094. The top 1% of the San Francisco peninsula's share of total income now extends to 30.8% of the region's income. This was a dramatic jump from 1989, where it stood at 15.8%."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The region's economy has been fundamentally transformed by the technology industry springing from Silicon Valley. Policies pushed by Mayor Ed Lee provided tax breaks for tech companies to set up shop along the city's long-neglected Mid-Market area. The city is now home to Twitter, Uber, Airbnb, Pinterest, Dropbox and others. In short, the Bay Area has become a global magnet for those with specialized skills, which has in turn helped fuel economic enthusiasm, and this economic growth has reduced unemployment to 3.4%, an admirable feat."}, {"label": "E", "text": "In spite of all that, the strength of the recent job growth, combined with policies that have traditionally limited housing development in the city and throughout the peninsula, did not help ease the affordability crisis. In 2015 alone, the Bay Area added 64,000 in jobs. In the same year, only 5,000 new homes were built."}, {"label": "F", "text": "With the average house in the city costing over $1.25 million and average flat prices over $1.11 million, the minimum qualifying income to purchase a house has increased to $254,000. Considering that the average household income in the city currently stands at around $80,000, it is not an exaggeration to say that the dream of home ownership is now beyond the grasp of the vast majority of today's people who rent."}, {"label": "G", "text": "For generations, the stability and prosperity of the American middle class have been anchored by home ownership. Studies have consistently shown that the value of land has overtaken overall income growth, thus providing a huge advantage to property owners as a vehicle of wealth building. When home prices soar above the reach of most households, the gap between the rich and the poor dramatically increases."}, {"label": "H", "text": "If contributing factors leading to housing becoming less than affordable are not resolved over multiple generations, a small elite will control a vast share of the country's total wealth. The result? A society where the threat of class warfare would loom large. A society's level of happiness is tied less to measures of quantitative wealth and more to measures of qualitative wealth. This means that how a person judges their security in comparison to their neighbors' has more of an impact on their happiness than their objective standard of living. At the same time, when a system no longer provides opportunities for the majority to participate in wealth building, it not only robs those who are excluded from opportunities, but also deprives them of their dignity."}, {"label": "I", "text": "San Francisco and the Bay Area have long been committed to values which embrace inclusion and rejection of mainstream culture. To see these values coming apart so publicly adds insult to injury for a region once defined by its progressive social fabric. In the face of resentment, it is human to want revenge. But deteriorating policies such as heavily taxing technology companies or real estate developers are not likely to shift the balance."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The housing crisis is caused by two primary factors: the growing desirability of the Bay Area as a place to live due to its excellent economy, and our limited housing stock. Although the city is experiencing an unprecedented boom in new housing, more units are sorely needed. Protection policies were originally designed to suppress bad development and boost historic preservation in our urban areas. Now, too many developers are experiencing excessive delays. Meanwhile, there are the land limitations of the Bay Area to consider. The region is surrounded by water and mountains. Local governments need to aid development as well. This means increasing housing density throughout the region and building upwards while streamlining the approval process."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Real estate alone will not solve the problem, of course. Transportation, too, needs to be updated and infrastructure extended to link distant regions to Silicon Valley and the city. We need to build an effective high-speed commuting system linking the high-priced and crowded Bay Area with the low-priced and low-density Central Valley. This would dramatically reduce travel times. And based on the operating speeds of hovering trains used in countries such as Japan or Spain, high-speed rail could shorten the time to travel between San Francisco and California's capital, Sacramento, or from Stockton to San Jose, to under 30 minutes. This system would bring once distant regions within reasonable commute to heavy job centers. The city also needs to update existing transportation routes combined with smart home-building policies that dramatically increase housing density in areas surrounding high-speed rail stations. By doing so, we will be able to build affordable housing within acceptable commuting distances for a significant bulk of the workforce."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Our threatening housing crisis forces the difficult question of what type of society we would like to be. Will it be one where the elite command the vast bulk of wealth and regional culture is defined by an aggressive business world? We were recently treated to a taste of the latter, when local tech employee Justin Keller wrote an open letter to the city complaining about having to see homeless people on his way to work."}, {"label": "M", "text": "It doesn't have to be this way. But solutions need to be implemented now, before angry crowds grow from a nuisance to serious concern. It may take less than you might think. And in fact, the solutions to our housing crisis are already fairly clear. We need to increase the density of housing units. We need to use existing technology to shorten travel times and break the land limits. There is a way to solve complex social and economic problems without abandoning social responsibility. This is the Bay Area's opportunity to prove that it can innovate more than just technology."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The ability to make inferences from same and different, once thought to be unique to humans, is viewed as a cornerstone of abstract intelligent thought. A new study, however, has shown that what psychologists call same-different discrimination is present in creatures generally seen as unintelligent: newborn ducklings(小鸭).\nThe study, published Thursday in Science, challenges our idea of what it means to have a birdbrain, said Edward Wasserman, an experimental psychologist at the University of Iowa who wrote an independent review of the study.\n\"In fact, birds are extremely intelligent and our problem pretty much lies in figuring out how to get them to 'talk' to us, or tell us how smart they really are,\" he said.\nAntone Martinho and Alex Kacelnik, co-authors of the new paper, devised a clever experiment to better test bird intelligence.\nFirst, they took 1-day-old ducklings and exposed them to a pair of moving objects. The two objects were either the same or different in shape or color. Then they exposed each duckling to two entirely new pairs of moving objects.\nThe researchers found that about 70% of the ducklings preferred to move toward the pair of objects that had the same shape or color relationship as the first objects they saw. A duckling that was first shown two green spheres, in other words, was more likely to move toward a pair of blue spheres than a mismatched pair of orange and purple spheres.\nDucklings go through a rapid learning process called imprinting shortly after birth—it's what allows them to identify and follow their mothers.\nThese findings suggest that ducklings use abstract relationships between sensory inputs like color, shape, sounds and odor to recognize their mothers, said Dr. Kacelnik.\nBy studying imprinting, the authors of this study have shown for the first time that an animal can learn relationships between concepts without training, said Jeffrey Katz, an experimental psychologist at Auburn University who was not involved in the study.\nPrevious studies have suggested that other animals, including pigeons, dolphins, honeybees and some primates(灵长类动物), can discern same from different, but only after extensive training.\nAdding ducklings to the list—particularly untrained newborn ducklings—suggests that the ability to compare abstract concepts \"is far more necessary to a wider variety of animals' survival than we previously thought,\" Dr. Martinho said. He believes the ability is so crucial because it helps animals consider context when identifying objects in their environment.\nIt's clear from this study and others like it that \"animals process and appreciate far more of the intricacies in their world than we've ever understood,\" Dr. Wasserman said. \"We are in a revolutionary phase in terms of our ability to understand the minds of other animals.\"", "47": "The ability to make inferences from same and different, once thought to be unique to humans, is viewed as a cornerstone of abstract intelligent thought. A new study, however, has shown that what psychologists call same-different discrimination is present in creatures generally seen as unintelligent: newborn ducklings(小鸭).\nThe study, published Thursday in Science, challenges our idea of what it means to have a birdbrain, said Edward Wasserman, an experimental psychologist at the University of Iowa who wrote an independent review of the study.\n\"In fact, birds are extremely intelligent and our problem pretty much lies in figuring out how to get them to 'talk' to us, or tell us how smart they really are,\" he said.\nAntone Martinho and Alex Kacelnik, co-authors of the new paper, devised a clever experiment to better test bird intelligence.\nFirst, they took 1-day-old ducklings and exposed them to a pair of moving objects. The two objects were either the same or different in shape or color. Then they exposed each duckling to two entirely new pairs of moving objects.\nThe researchers found that about 70% of the ducklings preferred to move toward the pair of objects that had the same shape or color relationship as the first objects they saw. A duckling that was first shown two green spheres, in other words, was more likely to move toward a pair of blue spheres than a mismatched pair of orange and purple spheres.\nDucklings go through a rapid learning process called imprinting shortly after birth—it's what allows them to identify and follow their mothers.\nThese findings suggest that ducklings use abstract relationships between sensory inputs like color, shape, sounds and odor to recognize their mothers, said Dr. Kacelnik.\nBy studying imprinting, the authors of this study have shown for the first time that an animal can learn relationships between concepts without training, said Jeffrey Katz, an experimental psychologist at Auburn University who was not involved in the study.\nPrevious studies have suggested that other animals, including pigeons, dolphins, honeybees and some primates(灵长类动物), can discern same from different, but only after extensive training.\nAdding ducklings to the list—particularly untrained newborn ducklings—suggests that the ability to compare abstract concepts \"is far more necessary to a wider variety of animals' survival than we previously thought,\" Dr. Martinho said. He believes the ability is so crucial because it helps animals consider context when identifying objects in their environment.\nIt's clear from this study and others like it that \"animals process and appreciate far more of the intricacies in their world than we've ever understood,\" Dr. Wasserman said. \"We are in a revolutionary phase in terms of our ability to understand the minds of other animals.\"", "48": "The ability to make inferences from same and different, once thought to be unique to humans, is viewed as a cornerstone of abstract intelligent thought. A new study, however, has shown that what psychologists call same-different discrimination is present in creatures generally seen as unintelligent: newborn ducklings(小鸭).\nThe study, published Thursday in Science, challenges our idea of what it means to have a birdbrain, said Edward Wasserman, an experimental psychologist at the University of Iowa who wrote an independent review of the study.\n\"In fact, birds are extremely intelligent and our problem pretty much lies in figuring out how to get them to 'talk' to us, or tell us how smart they really are,\" he said.\nAntone Martinho and Alex Kacelnik, co-authors of the new paper, devised a clever experiment to better test bird intelligence.\nFirst, they took 1-day-old ducklings and exposed them to a pair of moving objects. The two objects were either the same or different in shape or color. Then they exposed each duckling to two entirely new pairs of moving objects.\nThe researchers found that about 70% of the ducklings preferred to move toward the pair of objects that had the same shape or color relationship as the first objects they saw. A duckling that was first shown two green spheres, in other words, was more likely to move toward a pair of blue spheres than a mismatched pair of orange and purple spheres.\nDucklings go through a rapid learning process called imprinting shortly after birth—it's what allows them to identify and follow their mothers.\nThese findings suggest that ducklings use abstract relationships between sensory inputs like color, shape, sounds and odor to recognize their mothers, said Dr. Kacelnik.\nBy studying imprinting, the authors of this study have shown for the first time that an animal can learn relationships between concepts without training, said Jeffrey Katz, an experimental psychologist at Auburn University who was not involved in the study.\nPrevious studies have suggested that other animals, including pigeons, dolphins, honeybees and some primates(灵长类动物), can discern same from different, but only after extensive training.\nAdding ducklings to the list—particularly untrained newborn ducklings—suggests that the ability to compare abstract concepts \"is far more necessary to a wider variety of animals' survival than we previously thought,\" Dr. Martinho said. He believes the ability is so crucial because it helps animals consider context when identifying objects in their environment.\nIt's clear from this study and others like it that \"animals process and appreciate far more of the intricacies in their world than we've ever understood,\" Dr. Wasserman said. \"We are in a revolutionary phase in terms of our ability to understand the minds of other animals.\"", "49": "The ability to make inferences from same and different, once thought to be unique to humans, is viewed as a cornerstone of abstract intelligent thought. A new study, however, has shown that what psychologists call same-different discrimination is present in creatures generally seen as unintelligent: newborn ducklings(小鸭).\nThe study, published Thursday in Science, challenges our idea of what it means to have a birdbrain, said Edward Wasserman, an experimental psychologist at the University of Iowa who wrote an independent review of the study.\n\"In fact, birds are extremely intelligent and our problem pretty much lies in figuring out how to get them to 'talk' to us, or tell us how smart they really are,\" he said.\nAntone Martinho and Alex Kacelnik, co-authors of the new paper, devised a clever experiment to better test bird intelligence.\nFirst, they took 1-day-old ducklings and exposed them to a pair of moving objects. The two objects were either the same or different in shape or color. Then they exposed each duckling to two entirely new pairs of moving objects.\nThe researchers found that about 70% of the ducklings preferred to move toward the pair of objects that had the same shape or color relationship as the first objects they saw. A duckling that was first shown two green spheres, in other words, was more likely to move toward a pair of blue spheres than a mismatched pair of orange and purple spheres.\nDucklings go through a rapid learning process called imprinting shortly after birth—it's what allows them to identify and follow their mothers.\nThese findings suggest that ducklings use abstract relationships between sensory inputs like color, shape, sounds and odor to recognize their mothers, said Dr. Kacelnik.\nBy studying imprinting, the authors of this study have shown for the first time that an animal can learn relationships between concepts without training, said Jeffrey Katz, an experimental psychologist at Auburn University who was not involved in the study.\nPrevious studies have suggested that other animals, including pigeons, dolphins, honeybees and some primates(灵长类动物), can discern same from different, but only after extensive training.\nAdding ducklings to the list—particularly untrained newborn ducklings—suggests that the ability to compare abstract concepts \"is far more necessary to a wider variety of animals' survival than we previously thought,\" Dr. Martinho said. He believes the ability is so crucial because it helps animals consider context when identifying objects in their environment.\nIt's clear from this study and others like it that \"animals process and appreciate far more of the intricacies in their world than we've ever understood,\" Dr. Wasserman said. \"We are in a revolutionary phase in terms of our ability to understand the minds of other animals.\"", "50": "The ability to make inferences from same and different, once thought to be unique to humans, is viewed as a cornerstone of abstract intelligent thought. A new study, however, has shown that what psychologists call same-different discrimination is present in creatures generally seen as unintelligent: newborn ducklings(小鸭).\nThe study, published Thursday in Science, challenges our idea of what it means to have a birdbrain, said Edward Wasserman, an experimental psychologist at the University of Iowa who wrote an independent review of the study.\n\"In fact, birds are extremely intelligent and our problem pretty much lies in figuring out how to get them to 'talk' to us, or tell us how smart they really are,\" he said.\nAntone Martinho and Alex Kacelnik, co-authors of the new paper, devised a clever experiment to better test bird intelligence.\nFirst, they took 1-day-old ducklings and exposed them to a pair of moving objects. The two objects were either the same or different in shape or color. Then they exposed each duckling to two entirely new pairs of moving objects.\nThe researchers found that about 70% of the ducklings preferred to move toward the pair of objects that had the same shape or color relationship as the first objects they saw. A duckling that was first shown two green spheres, in other words, was more likely to move toward a pair of blue spheres than a mismatched pair of orange and purple spheres.\nDucklings go through a rapid learning process called imprinting shortly after birth—it's what allows them to identify and follow their mothers.\nThese findings suggest that ducklings use abstract relationships between sensory inputs like color, shape, sounds and odor to recognize their mothers, said Dr. Kacelnik.\nBy studying imprinting, the authors of this study have shown for the first time that an animal can learn relationships between concepts without training, said Jeffrey Katz, an experimental psychologist at Auburn University who was not involved in the study.\nPrevious studies have suggested that other animals, including pigeons, dolphins, honeybees and some primates(灵长类动物), can discern same from different, but only after extensive training.\nAdding ducklings to the list—particularly untrained newborn ducklings—suggests that the ability to compare abstract concepts \"is far more necessary to a wider variety of animals' survival than we previously thought,\" Dr. Martinho said. He believes the ability is so crucial because it helps animals consider context when identifying objects in their environment.\nIt's clear from this study and others like it that \"animals process and appreciate far more of the intricacies in their world than we've ever understood,\" Dr. Wasserman said. \"We are in a revolutionary phase in terms of our ability to understand the minds of other animals.\"", "51": "The suggestion that people should aim for dietary diversity by trying to eat a variety of foods has been a basic public health recommendation for decades in the United States and elsewhere. Now, however, experts are warning that aiming for a diverse diet may actually lead to just eating more calories, and, thus, to obesity. One issue is that people may not interpret \"variety\" the way nutritionists intend. This problem is highlighted by new research conducted by the American Heart Association. Researchers reviewed all the evidence published related to dietary diversity and saw a correlation between dietary diversity and a greater intake of both healthy and unhealthy foods. This had implications for obesity, as researchers found a greater prevalence of obesity amongst people with a greater dietary diversity.\nOne author of the new study explained that their findings contradict standard dietary advice, as most dietary guidelines around the world include a statement of eating a variety of foods. But this advice does not seem to be supported by science, possibly because there is little agreement about the meaning of \"dietary diversity,\" which is not clearly and consistently defined. Some experts measure dietary diversity by counting the number of food groups eaten, while others look at the distribution of calories across individual foods, and still others measure how different the foods eaten are from each other.\nAlthough the findings of this new study contradict standard dietary advice, they do not come as a surprise to all of the researchers involved. Dr. Rao, one of the study authors, noted that, after 20 years of experience in the field of obesity, he has observed that people who have a regimented lifestyle and diet tend to be thinner and healthier than people with a wide variety of consumption. This anecdotal evidence matches the conclusions of the study, which found no evidence that dietary diversity promotes healthy body weight or optimal eating patterns, and limited evidence that eating a variety of foods is actually associated with consuming more calories, poor eating patterns and weight gain. Further, there is some evidence that a greater variety of food options in a single meal may delay people's feeling of fullness and actually increase how much they eat.\nBased on their findings, the researchers endorse a diet consisting of a limited number of healthy foods such as vegetables, fruits, grains, and poultry. They also recommend that people simultaneously endeavor to restrict consumption of sweets, sugar and red meat. The researchers stress, however, that their dietary recommendations do not imply dietary diversity is never positive, and that, in the past, diversity in diets of whole, unprocessed food may have actually been very beneficial.", "52": "The suggestion that people should aim for dietary diversity by trying to eat a variety of foods has been a basic public health recommendation for decades in the United States and elsewhere. Now, however, experts are warning that aiming for a diverse diet may actually lead to just eating more calories, and, thus, to obesity. One issue is that people may not interpret \"variety\" the way nutritionists intend. This problem is highlighted by new research conducted by the American Heart Association. Researchers reviewed all the evidence published related to dietary diversity and saw a correlation between dietary diversity and a greater intake of both healthy and unhealthy foods. This had implications for obesity, as researchers found a greater prevalence of obesity amongst people with a greater dietary diversity.\nOne author of the new study explained that their findings contradict standard dietary advice, as most dietary guidelines around the world include a statement of eating a variety of foods. But this advice does not seem to be supported by science, possibly because there is little agreement about the meaning of \"dietary diversity,\" which is not clearly and consistently defined. Some experts measure dietary diversity by counting the number of food groups eaten, while others look at the distribution of calories across individual foods, and still others measure how different the foods eaten are from each other.\nAlthough the findings of this new study contradict standard dietary advice, they do not come as a surprise to all of the researchers involved. Dr. Rao, one of the study authors, noted that, after 20 years of experience in the field of obesity, he has observed that people who have a regimented lifestyle and diet tend to be thinner and healthier than people with a wide variety of consumption. This anecdotal evidence matches the conclusions of the study, which found no evidence that dietary diversity promotes healthy body weight or optimal eating patterns, and limited evidence that eating a variety of foods is actually associated with consuming more calories, poor eating patterns and weight gain. Further, there is some evidence that a greater variety of food options in a single meal may delay people's feeling of fullness and actually increase how much they eat.\nBased on their findings, the researchers endorse a diet consisting of a limited number of healthy foods such as vegetables, fruits, grains, and poultry. They also recommend that people simultaneously endeavor to restrict consumption of sweets, sugar and red meat. The researchers stress, however, that their dietary recommendations do not imply dietary diversity is never positive, and that, in the past, diversity in diets of whole, unprocessed food may have actually been very beneficial.", "53": "The suggestion that people should aim for dietary diversity by trying to eat a variety of foods has been a basic public health recommendation for decades in the United States and elsewhere. Now, however, experts are warning that aiming for a diverse diet may actually lead to just eating more calories, and, thus, to obesity. One issue is that people may not interpret \"variety\" the way nutritionists intend. This problem is highlighted by new research conducted by the American Heart Association. Researchers reviewed all the evidence published related to dietary diversity and saw a correlation between dietary diversity and a greater intake of both healthy and unhealthy foods. This had implications for obesity, as researchers found a greater prevalence of obesity amongst people with a greater dietary diversity.\nOne author of the new study explained that their findings contradict standard dietary advice, as most dietary guidelines around the world include a statement of eating a variety of foods. But this advice does not seem to be supported by science, possibly because there is little agreement about the meaning of \"dietary diversity,\" which is not clearly and consistently defined. Some experts measure dietary diversity by counting the number of food groups eaten, while others look at the distribution of calories across individual foods, and still others measure how different the foods eaten are from each other.\nAlthough the findings of this new study contradict standard dietary advice, they do not come as a surprise to all of the researchers involved. Dr. Rao, one of the study authors, noted that, after 20 years of experience in the field of obesity, he has observed that people who have a regimented lifestyle and diet tend to be thinner and healthier than people with a wide variety of consumption. This anecdotal evidence matches the conclusions of the study, which found no evidence that dietary diversity promotes healthy body weight or optimal eating patterns, and limited evidence that eating a variety of foods is actually associated with consuming more calories, poor eating patterns and weight gain. Further, there is some evidence that a greater variety of food options in a single meal may delay people's feeling of fullness and actually increase how much they eat.\nBased on their findings, the researchers endorse a diet consisting of a limited number of healthy foods such as vegetables, fruits, grains, and poultry. They also recommend that people simultaneously endeavor to restrict consumption of sweets, sugar and red meat. The researchers stress, however, that their dietary recommendations do not imply dietary diversity is never positive, and that, in the past, diversity in diets of whole, unprocessed food may have actually been very beneficial.", "54": "The suggestion that people should aim for dietary diversity by trying to eat a variety of foods has been a basic public health recommendation for decades in the United States and elsewhere. Now, however, experts are warning that aiming for a diverse diet may actually lead to just eating more calories, and, thus, to obesity. One issue is that people may not interpret \"variety\" the way nutritionists intend. This problem is highlighted by new research conducted by the American Heart Association. Researchers reviewed all the evidence published related to dietary diversity and saw a correlation between dietary diversity and a greater intake of both healthy and unhealthy foods. This had implications for obesity, as researchers found a greater prevalence of obesity amongst people with a greater dietary diversity.\nOne author of the new study explained that their findings contradict standard dietary advice, as most dietary guidelines around the world include a statement of eating a variety of foods. But this advice does not seem to be supported by science, possibly because there is little agreement about the meaning of \"dietary diversity,\" which is not clearly and consistently defined. Some experts measure dietary diversity by counting the number of food groups eaten, while others look at the distribution of calories across individual foods, and still others measure how different the foods eaten are from each other.\nAlthough the findings of this new study contradict standard dietary advice, they do not come as a surprise to all of the researchers involved. Dr. Rao, one of the study authors, noted that, after 20 years of experience in the field of obesity, he has observed that people who have a regimented lifestyle and diet tend to be thinner and healthier than people with a wide variety of consumption. This anecdotal evidence matches the conclusions of the study, which found no evidence that dietary diversity promotes healthy body weight or optimal eating patterns, and limited evidence that eating a variety of foods is actually associated with consuming more calories, poor eating patterns and weight gain. Further, there is some evidence that a greater variety of food options in a single meal may delay people's feeling of fullness and actually increase how much they eat.\nBased on their findings, the researchers endorse a diet consisting of a limited number of healthy foods such as vegetables, fruits, grains, and poultry. They also recommend that people simultaneously endeavor to restrict consumption of sweets, sugar and red meat. The researchers stress, however, that their dietary recommendations do not imply dietary diversity is never positive, and that, in the past, diversity in diets of whole, unprocessed food may have actually been very beneficial.", "55": "The suggestion that people should aim for dietary diversity by trying to eat a variety of foods has been a basic public health recommendation for decades in the United States and elsewhere. Now, however, experts are warning that aiming for a diverse diet may actually lead to just eating more calories, and, thus, to obesity. One issue is that people may not interpret \"variety\" the way nutritionists intend. This problem is highlighted by new research conducted by the American Heart Association. Researchers reviewed all the evidence published related to dietary diversity and saw a correlation between dietary diversity and a greater intake of both healthy and unhealthy foods. This had implications for obesity, as researchers found a greater prevalence of obesity amongst people with a greater dietary diversity.\nOne author of the new study explained that their findings contradict standard dietary advice, as most dietary guidelines around the world include a statement of eating a variety of foods. But this advice does not seem to be supported by science, possibly because there is little agreement about the meaning of \"dietary diversity,\" which is not clearly and consistently defined. Some experts measure dietary diversity by counting the number of food groups eaten, while others look at the distribution of calories across individual foods, and still others measure how different the foods eaten are from each other.\nAlthough the findings of this new study contradict standard dietary advice, they do not come as a surprise to all of the researchers involved. Dr. Rao, one of the study authors, noted that, after 20 years of experience in the field of obesity, he has observed that people who have a regimented lifestyle and diet tend to be thinner and healthier than people with a wide variety of consumption. This anecdotal evidence matches the conclusions of the study, which found no evidence that dietary diversity promotes healthy body weight or optimal eating patterns, and limited evidence that eating a variety of foods is actually associated with consuming more calories, poor eating patterns and weight gain. Further, there is some evidence that a greater variety of food options in a single meal may delay people's feeling of fullness and actually increase how much they eat.\nBased on their findings, the researchers endorse a diet consisting of a limited number of healthy foods such as vegetables, fruits, grains, and poultry. They also recommend that people simultaneously endeavor to restrict consumption of sweets, sugar and red meat. The researchers stress, however, that their dietary recommendations do not imply dietary diversity is never positive, and that, in the past, diversity in diets of whole, unprocessed food may have actually been very beneficial."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-03-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "Where does the conversation most probably take place?", "options": ["A) In a restaurant.", "B) In a kitchen.", "C) In a food store.", "D) In a supermarket."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题） In a restaurant.\n【定位】But one of my close friends ate here last week. She's a vegetarian. She told me that there were\na lot of options for her, and they offered alternative dishes.\n不过我一个好朋友上周在这儿吃过。她是素食者。她跟我说这里给她的选择很多，还提供替\n代菜品。\n【信号】menu、ate here、offered dishes 等餐厅标志词。环境标志词成串出现：第1段 I've\nbeen wanting to try this place、第2段 the menu 与 they offered alternative dishes、\n第10段 What are you going to have?——「菜单、上菜、点什么」只可能发生在餐厅，\n多个标志词共同锁定地点。\n【替换】the menu ↔ In a restaurant.\n有菜单、有人上菜的场所即餐厅\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nkitchen（厨房）借的是 dishes、menu 引发的「跟做饭有关」联想，但全程是别人给两人\n上菜、问他们点什么，没人在自己下厨，地点不是厨房。\n借用原文：they offered alternative dishes\nC) 原词陷阱\nfood store（食品店）只是蹭了满篇的食物话题，可对话里是坐下来看菜单、被问点什么，\n没有任何挑选购买商品的情节，与食品店不符。\nD) 原词陷阱\nsupermarket（超市）与 food store 错法相同，只因话题是吃就被列进来，但录音里\nWhat are you going to have? 是点餐、不是购物，排除。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman?", "options": ["A) She eats meat occasionally.", "B) She enjoys cheeseburgers.", "C) She is allergic to seafood.", "D) She is a partial vegetarian."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is a partial vegetarian.\n【定位】Well, not entirely. I don't eat meat at all, but I don't have a problem eating fish, seafood\nor eggs.\n嗯，不完全是。我完全不吃肉，但吃鱼、海鲜或蛋是没问题的。\n【信号】被问者应答 + not entirely 让步 + but 转折。男士问 you're not vegetarian, are you?，\n女士的应答即落点；她先说 not entirely（不完全是），再用 but 把「不吃肉」和「吃鱼\n蛋」对接——这种「部分是、不完全是」正是 partial vegetarian 的定义。\n【替换】I don't eat meat at all, but I don't have a problem eating fish, seafood or eggs ↔\nShe is a partial vegetarian.\n「不吃肉但吃鱼蛋」概括为「部分素食者」\nnot entirely ↔ partial vegetarian\n「不完全是」对应「部分」\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nShe eats meat occasionally.（偶尔吃肉）与原文 I don't eat meat at all（完全不吃肉）\n正好相反，把「一点都不吃」反成了「偶尔吃」。\n借用原文：I don't eat meat at all\nB) 张冠李戴\nShe enjoys cheeseburgers. 盗用了男士那句 bacon cheeseburgers，但说舍不得汉堡的\n是男士、不是女士，把男方的话安到了女方头上。\n借用原文：bacon cheeseburgers\nC) 原词陷阱\nShe is allergic to seafood. 盗用了 seafood 一词，但原文是她「吃海鲜没问题」，根本没\n提过敏，allergic 系凭空捏造且与事实相反。\n借用原文：eating fish, seafood or eggs", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What would be a difficult thing to do according to the woman?", "options": ["A) Dealing with one's colleagues.", "B) Changing one's eating habit.", "C) Following the same diet for years.", "D) Keeping awake at morning meetings."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Changing one's eating habit.\n【定位】I bet it would be hard though, giving up something you're accustomed to eating.\n不过我敢说，要放弃一样你习惯了的吃食，是会很难的。\n【信号】评价词 hard 直接修饰 giving up… eating。it would be hard 里的 hard（难）直接评价\n后面的 giving up something you're accustomed to eating——「放弃一样你已习惯吃的\n东西」，评价词紧贴的那件事就是答案，正是「改变饮食习惯」。\n【替换】giving up something you're accustomed to eating ↔ Changing one's eating habit.\n「放弃习惯吃的东西」=「改变饮食习惯」\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nDealing with one's colleagues. 盗用了 deal with me 旁边的 colleagues，但原文是「戒\n咖啡后同事得忍受我很难受」，难的是戒咖啡这件事，不是「处理同事」。\n借用原文：deal with me at morning meetings\nC) 原词陷阱\nFollowing the same diet for years. 蹭了 My parents followed the same diet，可那是在\n讲她从小随父母吃同样的饭、并不觉得难，不是被评为「难做」的事。\n借用原文：My parents followed the same diet\nD) 原词陷阱\nKeeping awake at morning meetings. 借用了 morning meetings，但原文只说戒咖啡让\n她早会上状态差、同事难受，从没说「在早会保持清醒」是难事，系拼凑原词。\n借用原文：morning meetings", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say about her parents?", "options": ["A) They enjoy perfect health.", "B) They are both animal lovers.", "C) They only eat organic food.", "D) They are cutting back on coffee."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They enjoy perfect health.\n【定位】Both are in their 70s now, but eat organic as often as possible and take part in regular physical\nexercise. Neither of them has any health problems, and they hardly ever get sick.\n他们俩现在都七十多岁了，但尽量常吃有机食品，也定期参加体育锻炼。两人都没有任何健\n康问题，也几乎从不生病。\n【信号】强否定 Neither…any + hardly ever 收束健康结论。落点在 Neither of them has any\nhealth problems 与 they hardly ever get sick——双重否定 + hardly ever 把父母身体状\n况推到「完全没病、几乎不生病」的极佳状态，这是对父母的总体定论。\n【替换】Neither of them has any health problems, and they hardly ever get sick ↔\nThey enjoy perfect health.\n「都没病、几乎不生病」=「身体极佳」\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThey are both animal lovers. 借了 animal preservation 一词，但原文说父母 not\nterribly active or knowledgeable about animal preservation efforts（对动物保护既不\n热心也不懂），与「都是爱动物者」相反。\n借用原文：not terribly active or knowledgeable about animal preservation efforts\nC) 偷换概念\nThey only eat organic food. 盗用 eat organic，却把原文的 as often as possible（尽量\n常吃）偷改成 only（只吃），范围被绝对化，不符。\n借用原文：eat organic as often as possible\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey are cutting back on coffee. 盗用了 cutting back on coffee，但戒咖啡的是女士自\n己（I tried cutting back on coffee），父母从未减咖啡，把女士的事安到了父母身上。\n借用原文：I tried cutting back on coffee", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What was the manager's reason for giving little praise for the man's performance?", "options": ["A) The man got a poor evaluation from his colleagues.", "B) The man made little contribution to the company.", "C) The man paid attention to trivial things.", "D) The man had an attitude problem."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The man had an attitude problem.\n【定位】he bluntly told me that I had an attitude problem.\n他直截了当地告诉我，我有态度问题。\n【信号】被问者复述经理原话的直接引述句。女士问考评结果后，男士应答里 he bluntly told me\nthat I had an attitude problem 是对经理原话的直接转述，'他告诉我……'这种引述就是答\n案信号——经理给出的原因被原原本本复述在这句里。\n【替换】I had an attitude problem ↔ The man had an attitude problem.\n原话第一人称 I，选项换成第三人称 The man，意思不变\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文差评出自男士的 manager(见 your annual performance evaluation meeting with\nyour manager)，A 却说 a poor evaluation from his colleagues'来自同事'，张冠李戴改\n了评价来源，故错。\n借用原文：your annual performance evaluation meeting with your manager\nB) 未提及\n经理给的理由是态度问题，全程没说男士 made little contribution to the company'贡献\n少'，这是凭'表扬少'常识脑补出的原因，原文无据。\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文经理说 it's the little things I do and say，C 的 paid attention to trivial things 用\ntrivial(琐碎)勾住这个 little things，听着像。但原文 little things 指'惹经理不快的琐碎言行\n(即态度的体现)'，不是说男士'只关注琐事'，含义被偷换。\n借用原文：it's the little things I do and say", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say about some managers?", "options": ["A) They make unhelpful decisions for solving problems.", "B) They favor some employees' suggestions over others.", "C) They use manipulative language to mask their irrational choices.", "D) They reject employees' reasonable arguments for work efficiency."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They use manipulative language to mask their irrational choices.\n【定位】Some managers really adopt manipulative language to disguise the unreasonable choices they\nhave made.\n有些经理确实会采用操控性的语言，来掩饰他们做出的那些不合理的选择。\n【信号】女士发言中直接评价经理的核心陈述句。女士这段以 Some managers really adopt\nmanipulative language to disguise the unreasonable choices 直陈对经理的判断，主语\nSome managers、动词 adopt…to disguise 把'手段+目的'说全，是承载答案的评价句。\n【替换】adopt manipulative language ↔ use manipulative language\nadopt 换成 use，同义动词替换\ndisguise the unreasonable choices ↔ mask their irrational choices\ndisguise→mask、unreasonable→irrational 双重同义替换\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n原文批评的是经理'用话术掩盖不合理决定'，重点在'掩盖'这个语言行为；A 的 make\nunhelpful decisions for solving problems 只截取'决定不好'一层，丢了'操控语言'的核\n心，且 unhelpful 与原文 unreasonable 侧重不同，未答到点上。\n借用原文：disguise the unreasonable choices they have made\nB) 未提及\n原文没说经理 favor some employees' suggestions over others'偏心采纳某些建议'，这\n是凭'经理不公'的印象自造的指控，原文无对应句。\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文紧接的下一句是 They don't use the rational power of arguments to resolve\nissues'他们不用理性论证去解决问题'，D 的 reject employees' reasonable arguments\nfor work efficiency 盗用 rational/arguments 这两个原词，并自添 for work efficiency(为\n了效率)的动机，原文既没说'否决员工合理论点'，更没提'效率'，原词在意思被改向。\n借用原文：They don't use the rational power of arguments to resolve issues of conflict", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "According to the woman, what do some managers think of honesty?", "options": ["A) It is a must for rational judgment.", "B) It is more of a sin than a virtue.", "C) It is a good quality in the workplace.", "D) It is more important now than ever."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） It is more of a sin than a virtue.\n【定位】Honesty was deemed to be more of a sin than a virtue, since it made people\nfeel uncomfortable.\n诚实被认为更像是一种罪过而非美德，因为它让人感到不舒服。\n【信号】评价动词 deemed 引出的定性，是态度落点。女士用 Honesty was deemed to be more\nof a sin than a virtue，deemed(被认为是)后面跟的 a sin(罪)就是这些经理对诚实的定\n性，评价性动词后的内容正是态度题答案信号。\n【替换】more of a sin than a virtue ↔ more of a sin than a virtue\n此项罕见地原句照搬，反成正确项\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文把诚实判为 more of a sin than a virtue'更像罪'，是负面；A 的 It is a must for\nrational judgment'理性判断的必需品'是正面拔高，极性与原文相反。\n借用原文：Honesty was deemed to be more of a sin than a virtue\nC) 与原文相反\nC 的 It is a good quality in the workplace'职场好品质'是正面评价，而原文这些经理把诚\n实看作 a sin(负面)，褒贬相反；workplace 虽是全篇高频词，但拿它包装的却是与原意相反\n的正面判断。\n借用原文：Honesty was deemed to be more of a sin than a virtue\nD) 未提及\n原文谈的是诚实被看作罪/还是美德，从没说它 more important now than ever'现在比过\n去更重要'，时间今昔的维度原文完全没出现。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman think is the priority of many managers?", "options": ["A) Smoothing relationships in the workplace.", "B) Making rational and productive decisions.", "C) Focusing on employees career growth.", "D) Preserving their power and prestige."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Preserving their power and prestige.\n【定位】And retaining their power and prestige is their priority.\n而且，保住他们的权力和威望，才是他们的首要之务。\n【信号】对话收尾处带 priority 的结论句。临近结束，女士用 And retaining their power and\nprestige is their priority 直接点出 priority，以 And 收束全段批评、给出结论，正是答案\n信号——对话的总结判断落在这句。\n【替换】retaining their power and prestige ↔ Preserving their power and prestige.\nretaining 换成 preserving，同义动词替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文说经理首要是保权保威，从没说他们以 Smoothing relationships in the\nworkplace'维系职场关系'为先，这是凭好印象自造的目标，原文无对应。\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文多处用到 rational(如 They don't use the rational power of arguments)，B 的\nMaking rational and productive decisions'做理性高效决定'正是拿这个高频词设套；但全\n篇批评经理不理性、靠话术保权，把'理性决策'当成他们的首要恰恰相反。\n借用原文：They don't use the rational power of arguments to resolve issues\nC) 未提及\n原文通篇批评经理只顾自己权威，绝无说他们以 Focusing on employees career\ngrowth'员工职业成长'为先，这与结论句的'保权'完全背离，原文无据。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "Why does the speaker say athletes are often viewed as heroes?", "options": ["A) They show genius which defies description.", "B) They accomplish feats many of us cannot.", "C) They create very high commercial value.", "D) They bring great honor to their country."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They accomplish feats many of us cannot.\n【定位】Athletes are seen as heroes because they can do things that most of us can't do.\n运动员被视为英雄，是因为他们能做到我们多数人做不到的事。\n【信号】because 后紧跟原因。答案信号是因果连词 because，它后面 they can do things that\nmost of us can\\'t do 正是录音给出的理由，因果词后那一句就是答案落点。\n【替换】do things that most of us can\\'t do ↔ accomplish feats many of us cannot\n同义改写：do things=accomplish feats\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey show genius which defies description. 的天赋/无法描述在录音里没有对应表述；\n录音说的是做到常人做不到的事，并未夸张到天才到无法形容，属拔高式编造。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey create very high commercial value. 借了录音里 They get paid millions of dollars\n和广告板出现这层商业画面来诱选，但那是顺带描述、并非说话人给出的成为英雄的原因；\n题问 Why are they heroes，答案是能力而非商业价值，听到钱就选会中招。\n借用原文：They get paid millions of dollars for their efforts\nD) 未提及\nThey bring great honor to their country. 的 country/为国争光在整段录音里没出现过，\n是听力惯用的拔高型干扰，第一步预读时已提醒要警惕 country 是否真被提到。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the speaker say many high-profile players do?", "options": ["A) They take part in kids' extra-curricular activities.", "B) They work in spare time to teach children sports.", "C) They try to be positive role models to children.", "D) They serve as spokespersons for luxury goods."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They try to be positive role models to children.\n【定位】They raise money for charities and act as mentors, talking to student groups and volunteering\ntheir time to programs that help children keep off drugs and stay in school.\n他们为慈善募款、担任导师，与学生群体交流，并把时间投入到帮助孩子远离毒品、留在学\n校的项目中。\n【信号】主语承接后的行为列举。答案句承接上句 many high-profile players work hard to be\npositive role models to children，紧接着用 volunteering their time to programs that\nhelp children keep off drugs and stay in school 具体列举其作为，行为列举处即题问的\n落点。\n【替换】help children keep off drugs and stay in school ↔\nKeeping athletes away from drug or alcohol problems\nkeep off=keep away from 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey take part in kids\\' extra-curricular activities. 借录音里频繁出现的\nchildren/student groups 这层与孩子相关的画面诱选，但录音说的是 talking to student\ngroups、volunteering，并非 take part in 课外活动，动作对不上。\n借用原文：talking to student groups\nB) 未提及\nThey work in spare time to teach children sports. 利用录音里 children 一词及对孩子的\n帮助来诱选，但录音从未说他们利用业余时间教孩子体育，teach ... sports 与 spare time\n都是录音没有的添加。\n借用原文：help children keep off drugs and stay in school\nD) 与原文相反\nThey serve as spokespersons for luxury goods. 把这群正面榜样说成代言奢侈品，方向\n与录音里 work hard to be positive role models to children 的公益形象相反，且 luxury\ngoods 录音未提。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the speaker say can be difficult?", "options": ["A) Separating an athlete's professional life from their personal life.", "B) Preventing certain athletes from getting in trouble with the law.", "C) Keeping athletes away from drug or alcohol problems.", "D) Being super sports stars without appearing arrogant."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Separating an athlete's professional life from their personal life.\n【定位】Separating an athlete's professional and personal lives can be tough.\n把运动员的职业生活与私人生活分开，可能很难。\n【信号】tough 这一难易判断词。答案信号是 can be tough 里的 tough，它正是题干 difficult 的\n同义词，与它搭配的 Separating an athlete\\'s professional and personal lives 即题问的\n那件难事。\n【替换】Separating an athlete\\'s professional and personal lives ↔\nSeparating an athlete\\'s professional life from their personal life\n几乎原句，仅句式微调\ncan be tough ↔ can be difficult\ntough=difficult\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nPreventing certain athletes from getting in trouble with the law. 直接搬录音里 gets in\ntrouble with the law 的原话诱选，但录音是把触犯法律当作运动员会犯错的举例，并没说\n防止其触法是件难事，问的难事是分开两种生活。\n借用原文：When a sports star gets in trouble with the law\nC) 原词陷阱\nKeeping athletes away from drug or alcohol problems. 借录音 plagued with alcohol\nproblems 的醒目词诱选，但那是 Mickey Mantle 个人遭遇的举例，录音没把远离毒品酒\n精说成困难，与 tough 搭配的并非此事。\n借用原文：Mickey Mantle, who was plagued with alcohol problems\nD) 未提及\nBeing super sports stars without appearing arrogant. 的不显傲慢在录音里没有任何对\n应，arrogant 一词及相关意思整段未出现，属凭空编造的难处。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the speaker say about weddings?", "options": ["A) They always cost more than expected.", "B) They are joyous and exciting occasions.", "C) They should be paid up by the attendees.", "D) They are dreams coming true to the brides."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They always cost more than expected.\n【定位】We don't need to tell you that weddings can get expensive. Even with the most meticulous\nbudgeting, a few unexpected costs are bound to occur.\n不用我们说你也知道，婚礼会变得很烧钱。哪怕预算做得再精细，也难免冒出一些意料之外\n的开销。\n【信号】开篇总起句直接定调。篇章第一句 weddings can get expensive 就是开门见山的总起，紧\n接着 a few unexpected costs are bound to occur 进一步坐实\"花费会超出预料\"。开头总\n起句是篇章题最常见的答案信号，听到第一句定性就该锁定。\n【替换】a few unexpected costs are bound to occur ↔ cost more than expected\nunexpected costs = cost more than expected\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThey are joyous and exciting occasions.（欢乐激动的场合）听起来像婚礼常识，很诱\n人，但说话人开篇的定性是\"烧钱、超预算\"，全篇也是讲婚礼黄掉的糟心事，根本没说婚礼\n欢乐。这是用生活常识代替原文的典型坑。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey should be paid up by the attendees.（该由出席者付费）盗用了原文 asking her\nfriends and family to pay up to attend her wedding 里的 pay up、attend——但那是\nSusan 一个人的荒唐做法，不是 speaker 对\"婚礼\"的普遍看法。听到 pay up 就选，正中原\n词陷阱。\n借用原文：asking her friends and family to pay up to attend her wedding\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey are dreams coming true to the brides. 借用了原文反复出现的 dream wedding、\nbrides，听着顺耳。但原文的 dream wedding 是讽刺 Susan 那场最终变 nightmare 的婚\n礼，绝非说\"婚礼是新娘美梦成真\"。这是把后文的反讽细节误当成开篇定性。\n借用原文：the $60,000 dream wedding was put on permanent hold", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do we learn about the wedding of the Canadian woman Susan?", "options": ["A) It cost $60,000.", "B) It was cancelled.", "C) It had eight guests only.", "D) It was held in Las Vegas."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It was cancelled.\n【定位】Yes, the couple called off the wedding just days before their wedding vows. Since their\nguests refused to pay the $1,500 attendance fee, the $60,000 dream wedding was put on\npermanent hold.\n是的，这对新人在交换结婚誓言前几天取消了婚礼。由于宾客拒付 1500 美元的出席费，这\n场 6 万美元的梦想婚礼被永久搁置了。\n【信号】结局动词 called off / put on permanent hold。答案落在表\"取消\"的动作上：the\ncouple called off the wedding 与 put on permanent hold 直接交代了婚礼的最终结局。\n问\"这场婚礼怎么了\"，结局性动词就是定位信号，盖过其中的数字干扰。\n【替换】the couple called off the wedding ↔ It was cancelled.\ncalled off = was cancelled\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt cost $60,000. 里的 6 万美元录音确实念到了——the $60,000 dream wedding——但\n原文是说这场\"标价 6 万的梦想婚礼被搁置\"，并不是说它\"真的花掉了 6 万\"（婚礼根本没办\n成）。听到 $60,000 就选，正中数字原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the $60,000 dream wedding was put on permanent hold\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt had eight guests only. 借了原文 only eight people responded positively 里的\neight。但那\"八个人\"是\"积极回应邀请和付费要求的人数\"，而婚礼根本取消了、压根没有出\n席的宾客。把\"回应的八人\"偷换成\"到场八位宾客\"，是细节偷换。\n借用原文：only eight people responded positively to the wedding invitations and money requests\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt was held in Las Vegas. 盗用了原文地名 getting married in Las Vegas。但拉斯维加斯\n只是未婚夫\"提议\"的地点，且 Susan 当场否决了，婚礼根本没在那办、也没办成。地名被搬\n到错误的事实上，是张冠李戴式原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Susan's future husband suggested getting married in Las Vegas", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did Susan's maid of honor advise her to do?", "options": ["A) Postpone her wedding.", "B) Ask her friends for help.", "C) Keep to her budget.", "D) Invite more guests."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Keep to her budget.\n【定位】Her maid of honor told her to stick to the budget, as she was asking too much from\nher guests.\n她的伴娘劝她守住预算，因为她对宾客的要求太过分了。\n【信号】建议信号 told her to。Her maid of honor told her to stick to the budget 中的 told\nher to 就是\"建议\"信号，信号后紧跟的动作 stick to the budget 即答案所在。听建议题，\n认准 told sb to / advised 这类信号，落点就在其后的动词短语。\n【替换】stick to the budget ↔ Keep to her budget\nstick to = keep to\n【排除】A) 未提及\nPostpone her wedding. 听着合理（婚礼出问题了，推迟很常见），但伴娘说的是守预算，\n从没提推迟。婚礼最后是被取消（called off）而非推迟，考生易把\"取消\"的印象误当成伴娘\n建议\"推迟\"。\nB) 与原文相反\nAsk her friends for help. 恰恰反了：原文里 Susan 正是因为 asking too much from her\nguests（向宾客 / 朋友要太多）才闹崩，伴娘是劝她\"别再这么要了、守预算\"，而非劝\n她\"找朋友帮忙\"。方向完全相反。\n借用原文：she was asking too much from her guests\nD) 与原文相反\nInvite more guests. 与伴娘的本意相悖。伴娘嫌 Susan 对宾客要求太多、要她收着点（守\n预算），怎会让她再多邀客人？这是把建议方向取反的干扰项。\n借用原文：Her maid of honor told her to stick to the budget", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "How did Susan respond to her future husband's suggestion?", "options": ["A) She called it romantic.", "B) She welcomed it with open arms.", "C) She said she would think about it.", "D) She rejected it flatly."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） She rejected it flatly.\n【定位】The bride quickly shut down the idea, saying she did not want a wedding of gambling and\nheavy drinking.\n新娘很快否决了这个主意，说她不想要一场满是赌博和酗酒的婚礼。\n【信号】强否定动作 shut down + 理由。The bride quickly shut down the idea 里 shut down\n（直接否决）加上副词 quickly，语气强烈而干脆；后面 she did not want 的否定理由再次\n坐实拒绝。态度题就抓这种强否定动作 + 否定理由作为信号。\n【替换】quickly shut down the idea ↔ rejected it flatly\nquickly shut down = rejected flatly\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nShe called it romantic.（说它浪漫）是正面反应，与原文 shut down the idea 的干脆否\n决完全相反。原文里浪漫一词根本没出现，是凭\"婚礼\"联想出的诱选。\n借用原文：The bride quickly shut down the idea\nB) 与原文相反\nShe welcomed it with open arms.（张开双臂欢迎）是强烈接受，恰与 Susan 的强烈拒绝\n相对。这是把态度光谱拨到了相反一端的干扰。\n借用原文：quickly shut down the idea\nC) 偷换概念\nShe said she would think about it.（说她会考虑）是夹在接受与拒绝之间的中间态度，最\n像\"温和版\"答案。但原文是 quickly shut down——又快又决绝，没有任何\"再想想\"的余\n地。把强否定弱化成\"考虑\"，是程度偷改。\n借用原文：The bride quickly shut down the idea", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What has a new study revealed about temperature?", "options": ["A) It determines people's moods.", "B) It can impact people's wellbeing.", "C) It is closely related to people's emotions.", "D) It can influence people's personalities."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It can influence people's personalities.\n【定位】Now, a new study has provided evidence that temperature can influence\npeople's personalities.\n如今，一项新研究提供了证据，表明温度能够影响人的性格。\n【信号】Now 引出新结论。前一句说\"天气影响情绪\"早已是定论，紧接 Now, a new study\nhas provided evidence 用 \"Now\"（如今）把新旧对照开来——\"Now+新研\n究\"正是答案信号，新研究的新发现就在这句，落点是 temperature can influence\npeople's personalities。\n【替换】temperature can influence people's personalities ↔ It can influence people's personalities.\n近乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA\"决定人的情绪 moods\"盗用了原文 weather changes can affect people's moods 里的\nmoods，但那是\"早已确立的旧定论\"、且用的是 affect（影响）不是 determine（决\n定），并非\"新研究的发现\"，听到 moods 就选会掉进时间错位的坑。\n借用原文：weather changes can affect people's moods\nB) 未提及\nB 说温度\"影响人的福祉 wellbeing\"，可原文新研究讲的是影响\"personalities 性格\"，从没\n出现 wellbeing 这层；属凭\"听着合理\"硬填，原文无据。\nC) 答非所问\nC\"与情绪密切相关 closely related to emotions\"对应的是 weather changes can affect\npeople's moods 那条\"早已确立\"的旧知识，不是\"new study revealed\"的新发现；既错在\n时间（旧非新），\"closely\"也夸大了原文 affect 的程度。\n借用原文：It has long been scientifically established that weather changes can affect\npeople's moods.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker say about warmer climates?", "options": ["A) They make people more reproductive.", "B) They increase people's life expectancy.", "C) They tend to produce positive feelings.", "D) They may alter people's genes gradually."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They tend to produce positive feelings.\n【定位】Warmer climates also make people feel more positive in general.\n温暖的气候总体上还会让人感觉更积极。\n【信号】主语直点 warmer climates。这句主语就是题干路标词 Warmer climates，谓语 \"make\npeople feel more positive\" 正面回答\"温暖气候使人怎样\"，是直接应答式的答案句，无需\n转折也无需推断。\n【替换】make people feel more positive in general ↔ They tend to produce positive feelings.\n使人感觉积极=产生积极感受\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA\"使人更能生育 reproductive\"原文从未提及生育这一层，是凭\"暖→繁衍\"的常识硬推，无\n原文依据。\nB) 未提及\nB\"延长寿命 life expectancy\"听着像健康类结论，但原文谈温暖气候只说让人更积极、更友\n善，从未涉及寿命；属合理却无据。\nD) 张冠李戴\nD\"逐渐改变人的基因 genes\"盗用了原文 about 40% of a person's personality is\ndetermined by their genes 里的 genes，但原文 genes 是讲\"基因决定四成性格\"，根本\n不是说\"温暖气候改变基因\"；把别处的词安到本题对象上，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：about 40% of a person's personality is determined by their genes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What did the online personality tests suggest?", "options": ["A) The link between temperature and personality is fairly weak.", "B) People share many personality traits despite their nationalities.", "C) People in the same geographical area may differ in personality.", "D) The Americans are apparently more outgoing than the Chinese."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The link between temperature and personality is fairly weak.\n【定位】However, the findings were much stronger for the Chinese group than the Americans studied,\nwhich suggests that though temperature plays a role, it does not play a dominant role. The\neffects are fairly weak.\n然而，对中国组的研究结果比对所研究的美国人要强得多，这表明温度虽起作用，但并不起\n主导作用。其影响相当微弱。\n【信号】However 转折收束。However 这个转折词把前文\"温度越接近22度、性格越明显\"的正面铺\n陈一收，转折后的 it does not play a dominant role 和 The effects are fairly weak 才是\n研究的真正定论——转折后才是落点，正是答案信号。\n【替换】The effects are fairly weak ↔ The link between temperature and personality is fairly weak.\n影响弱=关联弱\nit does not play a dominant role ↔ fairly weak\n非主导=弱\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nB\"人们尽管国籍不同却共享许多性格特质\"，但原文 the findings were much stronger for\nthe Chinese group than the Americans 恰恰强调中美两组结果差别很大（中国组强得\n多），方向与\"跨国籍相似\"相反。\n借用原文：the findings were much stronger for the Chinese group than the Americans studied\nC) 答非所问\nC\"同一地理区域的人性格可能不同\"借用了原文 personality traits vary across geographic\nregions 关于地区差异的说法，但那是第三段的背景旧知识，且讲的是\"跨地区\"差异，不\n是\"测试 suggest\"的结论；本题问 tests 说明了什么，答案在第五段的强弱定性，不在这\n里。\n借用原文：personality traits vary across geographic regions\nD) 原词陷阱\nD\"美国人显然比中国人更外向\"盗用了原文测过的性格维度 outgoingness 和\nChinese/Americans 这些原词，但原文只说中国组的研究结果\"更强\"，从未比较两国谁更外\n向；是把听过的词拼成一个原文没下的结论。\n借用原文：outgoingness", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "Why does the speaker say the finding of a new national poll reveals a serious problem?", "options": ["A) The number of older Americans living alone is on the rise.", "B) Chronic loneliness does harm to senior citizens in particular.", "C) Correlations have been found between loneliness and ill health.", "D) A growing number of US seniors face the risk of early mortality."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Chronic loneliness does harm to senior citizens in particular.\n【定位】This is a serious problem as research shows that chronic loneliness is particularly harmful to\nsenior citizens.\n这是个严重的问题，因为研究显示长期的孤独对老年人尤其有害。\n【信号】原句直说 serious problem 加 as 因果。录音里 This is a serious problem as research\nshows that chronic loneliness is particularly harmful to senior citizens 用 a serious\nproblem 直接呼应题干的 serious problem，再用 as（因为）引出原因——题干问 Why，\n答案就在这个 as 因果句里。\n【替换】chronic loneliness is particularly harmful to senior citizens ↔\nChronic loneliness does harm to senior citizens in particular\n近义改写：harmful 换 does harm，particularly 换 in particular\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第1段确实念过 loneliness seems to be on the rise，选项 The number of older\nAmericans living alone is on the rise 顺手借了 on the rise 制造耳熟，但录音说的是孤独\n在上升，不是独居老人人数在上升，且这不是题干问的为何是严重问题，属听到原词就想选\n的陷阱。\n借用原文：loneliness seems to be on the rise\nC) 原词陷阱\n第1段念过 correlations between loneliness and an assortment of medical\nconditions，选项 Correlations have been found between loneliness and ill health 借\n此造耳熟。但这是讲座开场的背景铺垫，题干问的是新民调揭示的严重问题，那条具体落在\n第2段的尤其伤害老人，C 答非所问。\n借用原文：studies have long found correlations between loneliness and an assortment of\nmedical conditions\nD) 原词陷阱\n第2段念过 increasing their risk of early mortality，选项 A growing number of US\nseniors face the risk of early mortality 借走 early mortality 与 risk。但录音说的是长期\n孤独提高了老人早亡风险，并没说越来越多老人面临早亡风险（人数增长），D 把风险升高\n偷换成人数增多，答非所问。\n借用原文：increasing their risk of early mortality", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is the good news the speaker talks about?", "options": ["A) Medication is available for treating loneliness.", "B) Loneliness rarely results from living alone.", "C) Being busy helps fight loneliness.", "D) Loneliness is probably reversible."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Loneliness is probably reversible.\n【定位】But there is good news. It looks like loneliness can be reversed.\n但有个好消息。看起来孤独是可以被逆转的。\n【信号】转折 But 加提示词 good news。录音 But there is good news 用转折 But 加题干同词\ngood news 双重提示，紧接的 loneliness can be reversed 就是好消息的内容——转折后/\n提示词后的那句才是真正落点。\n【替换】loneliness can be reversed ↔ Loneliness is probably reversible\n词性转换：can be reversed 换 reversible\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第5段提到 medical problems，选项 Medication is available for treating loneliness\n借 medical 的耳熟造出有药可治。但录音只把 medical problems 列为孤独的诱因之一，\n从没说有药物能治疗孤独，属无中生有的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：medical problems\nB) 原词陷阱\n第4段念过 someone who lives alone，选项 Loneliness rarely results from living alone\n借 living alone 造耳熟。录音确实暗示独居未必孤独，但用 rarely（很少）把它说成了绝对\n判断，且这不是题干问的好消息那一句，答非所问。\n借用原文：someone who lives alone doesn't meet that definition\nC) 与原文相反\n第4段念过 a house full of busy people，选项 Being busy helps fight loneliness 借\nbusy 造耳熟。可录音恰恰是说住在一屋子忙人中间的人反而可能孤独，busy 在原文是孤独\n的反例，C 把它反着用成忙碌能对抗孤独，与原文相反。\n借用原文：someone in a house full of busy people does", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What is the key to solving the loneliness problem according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Living with one's children.", "B) Meeting social expectations.", "C) Meaningful social contact.", "D) Timely medical intervention."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Meaningful social contact.\n【定位】While finding solutions for loneliness is highly personal, research suggests the best\ninterventions are those that involve meaningful social contact at least once a week.\n尽管为孤独寻找解决办法因人而异，但研究表明，最好的干预是那些每周至少有一次有意义\n社交接触的方式。\n【信号】推荐信号 research suggests the best。录音 research suggests the best interventions\nare those that involve meaningful social contact 用 suggests 加 the best（最佳）双重\n推荐信号点名办法——题干问 the key，答案就在这个给推荐的句子里。\n【替换】meaningful social contact at least once a week ↔ Meaningful social contact\n概括截取：选项是原句的核心保留\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n第4段念过 moving them in with their children，选项 Living with one's children 借\nchildren 造耳熟。但录音明说和子女同住的老人反而更易孤独，把它当解决办法正好相反，\n是听到熟词就选的陷阱。\n借用原文：seniors who lived with their children were more likely to report feeling lonely\nB) 原词陷阱\n第5段念过 social expectations that haven't been fulfilled，选项 Meeting social\nexpectations 借 social expectations 造耳熟。但录音把它列为孤独的诱因之一，不是解决\n孤独的关键，B 答非所问。\n借用原文：social expectations that haven't been fulfilled\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音念过 the best interventions 和第5段的 medical problems，选项 Timely medical\nintervention 拼合 medical 与 intervention 造耳熟。但录音说最佳 intervention 是有意义\n的社交接触，不是医疗干预；medical 在原文是诱因，D 张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the best interventions are those that involve meaningful social contact", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn about the speaker before she created the community We Are Travel Girls?", "options": ["A) She had a successful career in finance.", "B) She wrote stories about women travelers.", "C) She made regular trips to Asian countries.", "D) She invested in several private companies."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She had a successful career in finance.\n【定位】Before I started We Are Travel Girls, I had a successful 10-year career in finance in London,\nwhere I advised private clients on their investments.\n在创办 We Are Travel Girls 之前，我在伦敦有过一段成功的、长达十年的金融职业生涯，\n在那里我为私人客户提供投资建议。\n【信号】时间信号 Before…对上题干。答案句以 Before I started We Are Travel Girls 开头，与题\n干的 before she created the community 时间状语完全对应，这个『Before…』就是定位\n信号，它领出的那句正是讲她创办社区之前的身份。\n【替换】a successful 10-year career in finance ↔ a successful career in finance\n几乎原句，只省去年限\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 She wrote stories about women travelers. 盗用了录音第一段 We collect and\npublish stories from women traveling all over the world 里的 stories 与 women，但那\n是她现在做的社区业务、且是『收集发布』而非她个人『写』，题目问的是『创办社区之\n前』，时间和动作都对不上。\n借用原文：We collect and publish stories from women traveling all over the world\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 She made regular trips to Asian countries. 借了录音 launch our Travel With Us\ntrips in Bali, Japan 里的 trips 和亚洲地名，但那是社区『正准备推出』的旅行项目（未\n来），不是她个人过去定期去亚洲，与题目问的『之前』不符。\n借用原文：launch our Travel With Us trips in Bali, Japan and Malawi\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 She invested in several private companies. 盗用录音 I advised private clients on\ntheir investments 里的 private 和 invest，但原文是她『为私人客户提供投资建议』，主\n语和动作都被偷换成『她自己投资了几家私人公司』，意思已变。\n借用原文：I advised private clients on their investments", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did the speaker's friend suggest they do in 2015?", "options": ["A) Buy a ranch.", "B) Start a blog.", "C) Travel round the world.", "D) Set up a travel agency."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Start a blog.\n【定位】In 2015, I was looking at ways to leave finance, and my best friend Vanessa, who grew up on\na ranch on the Central Coast of California, suggested starting a blog.\n2015 年，我正在寻找离开金融业的办法，我最好的朋友 Vanessa——她在加州中央海岸的\n一个牧场长大——建议（我们）开一个博客。\n【信号】年份 In 2015 + suggested 双信号。答案句同时出现题干两个关键信号：In 2015 对上题\n干的 in 2015，suggested starting a blog 里的 suggested 正对题干的 suggest，年份加\n建议动词双重锁定，这句就是答案句。\n【替换】suggested starting a blog ↔ Start a blog\n动词词形变化，意思不变\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Buy a ranch. 盗用录音 who grew up on a ranch on the Central Coast of\nCalifornia 里的 ranch，但 ranch 只是描述朋友在牧场长大的背景，没人提议『买牧场』，\n纯属听到 ranch 就选的陷阱。\n借用原文：who grew up on a ranch on the Central Coast of California\nC) 未提及\n选项 Travel round the world. 借了本篇反复出现的 travel 主题氛围，但录音里朋友 2015\n年的提议是开博客，从未提议『环游世界』，属未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 Set up a travel agency. 蹭了 travel 这个高频词，但录音里朋友提议的是 blog，后来\n她们转做 creating a community（社区），从没说要开『旅行社』，动作对不上。\n借用原文：we turned our attention to creating a community among these women", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the speaker advise people to do if they want to become travel writers?", "options": ["A) Work hard to attract attention from publishers.", "B) Gain support from travel advertising companies.", "C) Try to find a full-time job in the travel business.", "D) Create something unique to enter the industry."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Create something unique to enter the industry.\n【定位】To anyone thinking about becoming a travel writer, I would suggest they first try and look for\na unique way to enter the industry.\n对任何想成为旅行作家的人，我建议他们首先去寻找一种独特的方式进入这个行业。\n【信号】建议信号 I would suggest。答案句以 I would suggest they first try and look for a\nunique way to enter the industry 给出，I would suggest 是明确的建议信号词，对上题\n干的 advise，suggest 引出的内容就是她的忠告。\n【替换】look for a unique way to enter the industry ↔ Create something unique to enter the industry\nfind a way 概括为 create something\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Work hard to attract attention from publishers. 借了录音另一句建议 Be prepared\nto work hard 里的 work hard，但她说努力是为了把它变成全职生意，根本没提『吸引出\n版商注意』，是把真实出现的 work hard 嫁接到没说过的目的上。\n借用原文：Be prepared to work hard if you want to turn it into a full-time business\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 Gain support from travel advertising companies. 盗用录音 try not to rush to\nselling advertising spaces 里的 advertising，但原文是劝人『别急着卖广告位』，方向与\n『争取广告公司支持』相反，意思被反着用。\n借用原文：try not to rush to selling advertising spaces before you have created a\ndedicated audience\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 Try to find a full-time job in the travel business. 蹭了录音 turn it into a full-time\nbusiness 里的 full-time，但原文说的是把事业做成全职生意，不是叫人去找一份旅行业的\n全职工作，且这并非她对『如何入行』的核心建议。\n借用原文：if you want to turn it into a full-time business", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is the speaker's advice on winning a loyal audience?", "options": ["A) Attracting sufficient investment.", "B) Creating an exotic corporate culture.", "C) Avoiding too much advertising early on.", "D) Refraining from promoting similar products."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Avoiding too much advertising early on.\n【定位】Try not to rush to selling advertising spaces before you have created a dedicated audience. If\nyou post too many promotions early on, you will turn many people off.\n在你培养起一批忠实受众之前，不要急着去卖广告位。如果你早期发太多推广，会让很多人\n反感离开。\n【信号】否定建议 try not to + early on。答案句是 Try not to rush to selling advertising\nspaces before you have created a dedicated audience 和 If you post too many\npromotions early on, you will turn many people off，try not to 这个否定建议信号配合\nearly on（早期），正是讲如何留住受众的关键句。\n【替换】try not to rush to selling advertising spaces before you have created a dedicated audience ↔\nAvoiding too much advertising early on\ntry not to 等于 avoiding，时机对应 early on\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Attracting sufficient investment. 里 investment 让人联想第二段她的金融/投资背景\nadvised private clients on their investments，但讲『赢得受众』的这段从未谈吸引投\n资，属拿别处话题词来干扰。\n借用原文：I advised private clients on their investments\nB) 未提及\n选项 Creating an exotic corporate culture. 没有任何录音原句支撑，exotic corporate\nculture（异域企业文化）是凭旅行主题臆造出来的，与赢得受众的建议无关，属未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 Refraining from promoting similar products. 盗用录音 post too many\npromotions 里的 promotion，但原文劝的是『别在早期发太多推广』，问题在数量与时\n机，不是『别推类似产品』，similar products 是录音没有的偷换。\n借用原文：If you post too many promotions early on, you will turn many people off", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) scenario\n名词\n【词性槽】the more likely ___：形容词比较级后接单数名词作主语中心词\n【依据】scenario = 可能发生的情景，对应\"我们自我毁灭\"这种假设性结局\nthe more likely __(26)__, … is that we humans will destroy ourselves：与小行星、超级\n火山\"可能发生的情景\"作比较，\"可能性更大的情景\"就是人类自毁，是 scenario 的典型用法\n（the more likely scenario is that…）。\n【竞争词】F) evaporation evaporation 与上下文\"灾难情景\"无关\nevaporation 指物理\"蒸发\"，无法与 asteroids、super volcanoes 这些\"可能事件\"作平行\n比较。\nO) variety the more likely variety 不构成自然搭配\nvariety 表\"种类、多样性\"，是分类词，不指向某个\"具体可能事件\"，与 is that 引导的同位语从\n句\"具体事件\"不匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the more likely ___："]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) extinction\n名词\n【词性槽】human ___ risks：两名词之间夹一空，作 noun adjunct 修饰 risks\n【依据】human extinction risks = 人类灭绝风险，呼应全文 wiped from the face of the\nEarth 的主旨\n首段 humanity, every last person, could someday be wiped from the face of the\nEarth 已把全文核心议题定为\"人类灭绝\"；human extinction risks 是该议题在学术语篇里\n的标准表达，与 human __(27)__ risks are poorly understood 中\"被理解得很差\"的对象完\n全吻合；extinction 作 noun adjunct 前置修饰 risks，语法上完全合法（参照 climate\nchange risks 同类构造）。\n【竞争词】E) essential essential risks 语义模糊、不切主题\nessential 意为\"必不可少的\"，essential risks 不构成有意义的表达，更无法对应下文反复出现的\nexistential risks（生存性风险）这一主题词。\nF) evaporation human evaporation 与主题不符\nevaporation 表\"蒸发\"，与 human 组合无主流学术意义，也不与下文\"existential risks\"形成\n对应。\nH) obscure obscure 留给 29 题、且不修饰本句主语\n下文 But others are __(29)__ or even exotic 才会用到\"鲜为人知\"这层意思；本句要刻画的\n是\"人类面临的何种类别的风险被低估\"，应是题材性限定（灭绝），而非性质性修饰（模糊）。\nO) variety human variety risks 不成立\nvariety 几乎只能用在 a variety of 结构中，无法作 noun adjunct 给 risks 加题材限定。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— essential risks 听着像\"关键风险\"很诱人。human __(27)__\nrisks are poorly understood 处若只看本句、不回主旨，很容易误选 essential\"本质风\n险\"；防坑：始终回到首段 humanity, every last person, could someday be wiped from\nthe face of the Earth 这句话定题材，与 extinction 一一对应。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·human ___ risks：两名词之"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) severely\n副词\n【词性槽】worse still, ___ underestimated：修饰过去分词 underestimated\n【依据】severely underestimated = 被严重低估，与 poorly understood 程度副词平行\npoorly understood and, worse still, __(28)__ underestimated by society：前后并列结\n构，副词位都修饰被动分词，描述\"理解不充分\"与\"低估\"的负面程度；severely 与 poorly\n同色调、加重语气，呼应 worse still。\n【竞争词】M) shrewdly shrewdly 是褒义\"精明地\"，与 underestimated 的负面语境冲突\nshrewdly underestimated 等于说\"精明地低估了\"，自相矛盾，且与 worse still 表示的\"更\n糟\"色调相反。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— shrewdly 与 severely 都是副词，词性对、易混。worse\nstill, __(28)__ underestimated by society 处考生若只卡词性、不看色调，可能误选\nshrewdly；防坑：紧盯 worse still 这一句间连接词，色调一负到底。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·worse still, ___ und"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) obscure\n形容词\n【词性槽】others are ___ or even exotic：系动词 are 后接形容词作表语\n【依据】obscure 与 exotic 同色调：鲜为人知 → 甚至怪异\nBut others are __(29)__ or even exotic 与上句 Some of these existential risks are fairly\nwell known 形成\"有些众所周知，但另一些则……\"的转折；空处需与 \"well known\" 反义，\n与 exotic 同向，先\"鲜为人知\"再\"怪异\"层层递进，正是 obscure。\n【竞争词】E) essential essential 与 exotic 不同色、且与 well known 不构成反义\nessential 意为\"本质的、必要的\"，既无法与 exotic 形成程度递进，也无法与上句 well known\n构成转折，逻辑链断裂。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·others are ___ or ev"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) arise\n动词\n【词性槽】risks that ___ from：that 引导的定语从句中作谓语，后接 from\n【依据】arise from = 源自、由……产生，对应\"来自人类技术的风险\"\nthe subset of existential risks that __(30)__ from human technology 表达\"源自人类技\n术的那一类生存风险\"，arise from 是英语描述\"源自/由……引起\"的固定搭配，与主语 risks\n复数原形相配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·risks that ___ from："]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) posed\n动词\n【词性槽】risks ___ to humans by …：名词后接非谓语作后置定语\n【依据】pose a risk to sb by sth = 由某物对某人造成风险\nthe risks __(31)__ to humans by technological progress：pose 与\nrisk/threat/challenge 是英语里\"造成……威胁/风险\"的强搭配，且 to humans 与 by\ntechnological progress 分别充当 pose 的间接宾语方向与施事，被动式 posed 一招\n到位。\n【竞争词】C) emphasized emphasize 与 risk 不构成\"造成风险\"的语义\nemphasize 意为\"强调\"，emphasized to humans by 表达不出\"由技术进步带给人类的风险\"的\n因果关系；且 risks emphasized to humans 在逻辑上与\"Bostrom 担忧技术造成的风险\"主线\n偏离。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·risks ___ to humans"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) advocate\n名词\n【词性槽】a longtime ___ of：a 后接单数可数名词，of 引出对象\n【依据】an advocate of X = X 的倡导者，与上下文呼应\nhe is a longtime __(32)__ of trans-humanism—the effort to improve the human\ncondition…through technological means，前句 Bostrom is no luddite 说他不是科技进\n步反对者，正面身份就是\"长期倡导者\"，an advocate of trans-humanism 是固定搭配。\n【竞争词】D) encounter encounter 作名词义为\"遭遇\"，不指人\na longtime encounter of trans-humanism = \"超人类主义的一段长期遭遇\"，既不指人也无法\n被 of 紧跟一个学说作宾语，与前句\"他不是反对者\"的逻辑承接不上。\nF) evaporation evaporation 是物理过程词、不指人\nevaporation of trans-humanism 无意义，且无法承接\"he is\"的身份判定。\nO) variety a longtime variety of … 不指人，逻辑也不通\nvariety of 通常表\"……的多种类型\"，无法与 he is 构成身份关系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a longtime ___ of：a"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) particular\n形容词\n【词性槽】in ___ 介词后接名词或形容词，与 in particular 构成固定短语\n【依据】in particular = 尤其，引出后面的具体例子 probability theory\nin __(33)__, probability theory 后半段对前半句 the tools of philosophy and\nmathematics 中的 mathematics 作\"尤其是\"的细化举例，正是 in particular 的典型用法。\n【竞争词】E) essential in essential 不是英语固定搭配\nessential 意为\"本质的\"，常说 in essence（本质上）而非 in essential；放进 in __(33)__,\nprobability theory 既无搭配支撑、语义上也不能引出举例。\nH) obscure in obscure 不成搭配且语义反向\nobscure 是\"模糊/鲜为人知的\"，在此既无固定短语，也与 Bostrom\"明确使用概率论\"的语境\n冲突。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·in ___ 介词后接名词或形容词，与"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) species\n名词\n【词性槽】we as a ___：as a 后接单数可数名词，we 的同位\n【依据】we as a species = 我们作为一个物种，与全文\"人类整体存亡\"主旨严丝合缝\nhow we as a __(34)__ might achieve this safe passage 的语境是讨论\"人类作为一个整体\n如何安全跨越技术之桥\"，species（物种）正是把\"全人类\"集合化的标准学术用语，与首段\nhumanity, every last person 的视角一致。species 单复数同形，符合 as a 单数要求。\n【竞争词】F) evaporation evaporation 是\"蒸发\"，不能作 we 的同位身份\nevaporation 描述物理/抽象过程，与\"我们作为一个 X\"的身份位完全不搭。\nK) scenario scenario 留给 26 题且语义不通\nscenario 是\"情景\"，we as a scenario 不构成意义；并且 26 题（the more likely ___）正是为\nscenario 量身定做的位置。\nO) variety we as a variety 不成立\nvariety 指\"多样性、种类\"，搭配上多说 a variety of，无法独立作 we 的身份同位；且 a variety\n在此既无具体所指也偏离主题。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·we as a ___：as a 后接单"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocate", "B) arise", "C) emphasized", "D) encounter", "E) essential", "F) evaporation", "G) extinction", "H) obscure", "I) particular", "J) posed", "K) scenario", "L) severely", "M) shrewdly", "N) species", "O) variety"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) encounter\n动词\n【词性槽】humanity might ___：情态动词 might 后接动词原形\n【依据】encounter risks = 遭遇风险，是及物动词，宾语正是先行词 risks\nexistential risks that humanity might __(35)__ in the decades and centuries to come\n中，空是定语从句的谓语动词、需要及物动词以\"risks\"为宾语；encounter 正是\"遭遇/碰到\n（风险/困难）\"的标准搭配。\n【竞争词】B) arise arise 是不及物动词，不能直接带宾语 risks\narise 意为\"产生、出现\"，是不及物动词（risks arise，而不是 humanity arise risks）；放\n进\"humanity might arise risks\"语法立刻不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·humanity might ___：情"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "The higher rate of employment, combined with limited housing supply, did not make it any easier to buy a house.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】就业 + 供房不足却没让买房更易\n【定位】In spite of all that, the strength of the recent job growth, combined with policies that have\ntraditionally limited housing development in the city and throughout the peninsula, did not\nhelp ease the affordability crisis.\n尽管如此，近期就业增长的强劲势头，叠加传统上限制本市及整个半岛住房开发的政策，并\n没有帮助缓解可负担危机。\n【改写】higher rate of employment ↔ the strength of the recent job growth\n就业率升 = 就业增长势头强\nlimited housing supply ↔ policies that have traditionally limited housing development\n限制开发即压缩供给\ndid not make it any easier to buy a house ↔ did not help ease the affordability crisis\n可负担危机未缓解 = 买房没变容易\n【辨邻】 F F 段算的是 the minimum qualifying income to purchase a house has increased\nto $254,000 与 the average household income in the city currently stands at\naround $80,000 的差距，结论与买房难有关，但没有 higher rate of employment\n这条原因要素，因此不是本题来源。\nthe minimum qualifying income to purchase a house has increased to $254,000", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "One way to deal with the housing crisis is for the government to simplify approval procedures for housing projects.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】政府简化住房项目审批\n【定位】This means increasing housing density throughout the region and building upwards while\nstreamlining the approval process.\n这意味着要在整个地区提高住房密度、向上建造，同时精简审批流程。\n【改写】simplify approval procedures for housing projects ↔ streamlining the approval process\nstreamline = simplify，process = procedures\n【辨邻】 M M 段重在 We need to increase the density of housing units 与 use existing\ntechnology to shorten travel times and break the land limits 等动作，未出现\napproval 关联词，因此不是本题来源段。\nthe solutions to our housing crisis are already fairly clear", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Residents of the San Francisco Bay Area strongly resent the tech industry because of the economic inequality it has contributed to.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】居民因贫富差距强烈憎恨科技业\n【定位】While the residents of most cities display pride and support for their home industries, drastic\nmarket distortions in the San Francisco Bay Area have created boiling resentment in the\nregion towards the tech industry.\n尽管大多数城市的居民都为本地产业感到自豪并予以支持，旧金山湾区却因剧烈的市场扭\n曲，对科技业产生了强烈的怨气。\n【改写】strongly resent the tech industry ↔ boiling resentment in the region towards the tech industry\n动词 resent 与名词 resentment 同根；strongly = boiling\nbecause of the economic inequality it has contributed to ↔\ndrastic market distortions in the San Francisco Bay Area have created boiling resentment\n市场扭曲 = 经济不平等的具体表现\n【辨邻】 I I 段 In the face of resentment, it is human to want revenge 是对已有怨气的回\n应，没有把“怨气 → 科技业 → 经济不平等”这条归因链给出。\nIn the face of resentment, it is human to want revenge", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The fast rise in the prices of land and houses increases the economic inequality among people.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】地价/房价飙升扩大经济差距\n【定位】When home prices soar above the reach of most households, the gap between the rich and the\npoor dramatically increases.\n当房价飙升至大多数家庭难以企及时，贫富差距就会急剧扩大。\n【改写】fast rise in the prices of land and houses ↔\nWhen home prices soar above the reach of most households\nfast rise = soar\nincreases the economic inequality among people ↔\nthe gap between the rich and the poor dramatically increases\n经济不平等 = 贫富差距\n【辨邻】 C C 段聚焦 San Francisco ranks first in California for economic difference 与 The\naverage income of the top 1% of households in the city averages $3.6 million\n等收入差距数字，没有把“房价/地价上升”作为原因引入。\nSan Francisco ranks first in California for economic difference\nH H 段 If contributing factors leading to housing becoming less than affordable\nare not resolved over multiple generations, a small elite will control a vast share\nof the country's total wealth 是远期社会后果论，与陈述句要的直接因果不同。\na small elite will control a vast share of the country's total wealth", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "San Francisco city government offered tax benefits to attract tech companies to establish operations in a less developed area.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】市政府用税收优惠引科技公司去欠发达区\n【定位】Policies pushed by Mayor Ed Lee provided tax breaks for tech companies to set up shop\nalong the city's long-neglected Mid-Market area.\n由市长 Ed Lee 推动的政策为科技公司提供税收减免，让它们在该市长期被忽视的 Mid-\nMarket 区域开设业务。\n【改写】offered tax benefits ↔ provided tax breaks\noffered = provided，benefits = breaks\nto attract tech companies to establish operations ↔ for tech companies to set up shop\nto attract ... to establish = for ... to set up shop\nin a less developed area ↔ along the city's long-neglected Mid-Market area\nless developed = long-neglected\n【辨邻】 I I 段 deteriorating policies such as heavily taxing technology companies or real\nestate developers 描述的是与本题相反的政策方向，且语境是“不要这么做”。看到\ntax 就选会被“正话反说”诱导。\ndeteriorating policies such as heavily taxing technology companies", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Innovative solutions to social and economic problems should be introduced before it is too late.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】及时引入创新解法\n【定位】But solutions need to be implemented now, before angry crowds grow from a nuisance to\nserious concern.\n但解决方案必须立刻实施，趁愤怒的人群还没从小麻烦发展成严重问题。\n【改写】Innovative solutions to social and economic problems ↔\na way to solve complex social and economic problems without abandoning social responsibility\nsocial and economic problems 原词复现，Innovative ↔ innovate 同根\nbefore it is too late ↔\nsolutions need to be implemented now, before angry crowds grow from a nuisance to serious\nconcern\nnow + before sth gets worse = before it is too late\n【辨邻】 J J 段更像问题剖析与对策列举；陈述句“创新解法 + 趁早”是宏观呼吁，与 M 段的尾段\n定位匹配。\nincreasing housing density throughout the region and building upwards while streamlining the\napproval process\nK K 段聚焦 high-speed commuting system 这一具体方案，无 before it is too late 这\n种时间紧迫性表述。\nTransportation, too, needs to be updated and infrastructure extended to link distant regions to\nSilicon Valley and the city", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "When people compare their own living standard with others', it has a greater impact on their sense of contentment.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】和别人比较影响幸福感\n【定位】This means that how a person judges their security in comparison to their neighbors' has more\nof an impact on their happiness than their objective standard of living.\n这意味着，一个人如何与邻居比较自己的安全感，对其幸福感的影响要大于他客观的生活\n水平。\n【改写】compare their own living standard with others' ↔\nhow a person judges their security in comparison to their neighbors'\ncompare with others' = in comparison to neighbors'\nit has a greater impact on their sense of contentment ↔\nhas more of an impact on their happiness than their objective standard of living\nsense of contentment = happiness; greater impact = more of an impact\n【辨邻】 G G 段 When home prices soar above the reach of most households, the gap\nbetween the rich and the poor dramatically increases 是经济差距视角，缺少“幸福\n感/满足感”这层。\nthe gap between the rich and the poor dramatically increases", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "San Francisco has been found to have the biggest income gap in California between the rich and the poor.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】加州贫富差距最大的城市\n【定位】According to a recent study, San Francisco ranks first in California for economic difference.\n根据最近的一项研究，旧金山在加州的经济差距方面排名第一。\n【改写】biggest income gap in California ↔\nSan Francisco ranks first in California for economic difference\nranks first = biggest，economic difference = income gap\nbetween the rich and the poor ↔\nThe average income of the top 1% of households in the city averages $3.6 million. This is 44\ntimes the average income of those at the bottom\ntop 1% vs those at the bottom = rich vs poor", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Improved transport networks connecting the city to distant outlying areas will also help solve the housing crisis.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】改善交通连接远郊缓解住房危机\n【定位】Transportation, too, needs to be updated and infrastructure extended to link distant regions to\nSilicon Valley and the city.\n交通也需要升级，基础设施需要延伸，把远郊与硅谷及本市连接起来。\n【改写】Improved transport networks ↔\nTransportation, too, needs to be updated and infrastructure extended\nImproved = updated + extended\nconnecting the city to distant outlying areas ↔ link distant regions to Silicon Valley and the city\nconnect = link，distant outlying areas = distant regions\nwill also help solve the housing crisis ↔\nwe will be able to build affordable housing within acceptable commuting distances for a\nsignificant bulk of the workforce\n建可负担住房 = 帮助解决住房危机", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Average incomes in the Bay Area make it virtually impossible for most tenant families to buy a home.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】平均收入让大多数租户买不起房\n【定位】Considering that the average household income in the city currently stands at around $80,000,\nit is not an exaggeration to say that the dream of home ownership is now beyond the grasp of\nthe vast majority of today's people who rent.\n考虑到该市目前家庭平均收入约 8 万美元，说“如今对绝大多数租户而言，拥有住房的梦想\n已遥不可及”并不夸张。\n【改写】Average incomes in the Bay Area ↔\nthe average household income in the city currently stands at around $80,000\n平均收入两端同义\nvirtually impossible for most tenant families to buy a home ↔\nthe dream of home ownership is now beyond the grasp of the vast majority of today's people\nwho rent\nbeyond the grasp = virtually impossible；people who rent = tenant families\n【辨邻】 E E 段 did not help ease the affordability crisis 是结论性表述，无具体收入数字、无\n“租户群体”指向，与陈述句的算账型命题对不上。\ndid not help ease the affordability crisis\nG G 段聚焦 the value of land has overtaken overall income growth 与 the gap\nbetween the rich and the poor dramatically increases 的宏观机制，与陈述句的具\n体算账不同。\nthe value of land has overtaken overall income growth", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "In what way were humans thought to be unique?", "options": ["A) Being a major source of animal intelligence.", "B) Being the cornerstone of the creative world.", "C) Being capable of same-different discrimination.", "D) Being able to distinguish abstract from concrete."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】In what way were humans thought to be unique? 是 in what way 引导的客观信息问\n句，对应原文一句话即可回答。\n【拆句】Q46 是一个被动语态的疑问句，主干是 humans were thought to be unique，were\nthought 是过去时被动，提示这一观点是过去的传统看法（呼应原文 once thought to be\nunique to humans 中的 once）。in what way 问的是 unique 的具体维度。\n【定位】The ability to make inferences from same and different, once thought to be unique to humans,\nis viewed as a cornerstone of abstract intelligent thought.\n曾被认为是人类独有的、对 \"同\" 与 \"异\" 进行推断的能力，被视为抽象智力思维的基石。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项 Being a major source of animal intelligence 说人类是 \"动物智力的主要来源\"，\n原文从未提出这一观点；文章只对比人与动物的智力，没说人是动物智力的源头。\nB) 偷换概念\ncornerstone of abstract intelligent thought 修饰的是 \"同/异推断能力\"，是 \"抽象智\n力的基石\"，不是 \"创造性世界的基石\"；选项 Being the cornerstone of the creative\nworld 把对象偷换成 the creative world。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmake inferences from same and different ↔ same-different discrimination\nonce thought to be unique to humans ↔ Being capable of same-different discrimination\n预判 \"人类独有的是 same-different 推断能力\"，C 选项 Being capable of same-\ndifferent discrimination 把这一信息完整还原，且来源于原文同一句话，是定位句的标\n准同义改写。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只提及 abstract intelligent thought，从未提及 concrete 这一概念；选项 Being\nable to distinguish abstract from concrete 把抽象与具体编造成可区分的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn from the study published in Science?", "options": ["A) Our understanding of the bird world was biased.", "B) Our conception of birds' intelligence was wrong.", "C) Our communication with birds was far from adequate.", "D) Our knowledge about bird psychology needs updating."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the study published in Science? 限定了来源，问该研究告诉我\n们什么客观结论，对应原文该研究的总评句。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What do we learn from the study；过去分词短语 published in Science 后\n置修饰 the study，相当于定语从句 which was published in Science，用来锁定具体哪一\n项研究。\n【定位】The study, published Thursday in Science, challenges our idea of what it means to have a\nbirdbrain, said Edward Wasserman, an experimental psychologist at the University of Iowa\nwho wrote an independent review of the study.\nWasserman 表示，这项发表于周四《科学》杂志上的研究挑战了我们对 \"birdbrain\" 一词\n含义的认知。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n选项 Our understanding of the bird world was biased 把对象从 \"鸟的智力\" 扩大成\n\"鸟的世界\"——原文只挑战对鸟智力的看法，没说我们对整个鸟类世界的理解有偏见。\nB) ✓ 正确\nchallenges our idea of what it means to have a birdbrain ↔\nOur conception of birds' intelligence was wrong\nbirds are extremely intelligent and our problem pretty much lies in figuring out how to get them\nto 'talk' to us\n↔ Our conception of birds' intelligence was wrong\n预判 \"鸟脑认知被推翻\"，Our conception of birds' intelligence was wrong 与之严丝\n合缝；B 是定位句的标准同义改写。\nC) 答非所问\nour problem pretty much lies in figuring out how to get them to 'talk' to us 是\nWasserman 的延伸说明，不是该研究本身告诉我们的核心结论；选项 Our\ncommunication with birds was far from adequate 误把延伸内容当作主结论。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 Our knowledge about bird psychology needs updating 用 bird psychology\n替换 birds' intelligence，鸟类心理学是更广的学科范畴，且 needs updating 改变了\n原文 \"挑战\"（即推翻）的强度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What did the researchers discover about most ducklings from their experiment?", "options": ["A) They could associate shape with color.", "B) They reacted quickly to moving objects.", "C) They preferred colored objects to colorless ones.", "D) They could tell whether the objects were the same."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did the researchers discover about most ducklings from their experiment? 直\n接问研究者发现了什么，是对实验结果的客观提问。\n【拆句】主干是 What did the researchers discover；about most ducklings 是 discover 的内容\n限定，from their experiment 是来源状语。整句无陷阱，按 \"实验 → 发现 → 关于多数小\n鸭的结论\" 这一链条去原文找即可。\n【定位】The researchers found that about 70% of the ducklings preferred to move toward the pair of\nobjects that had the same shape or color relationship as the first objects they saw.\n研究者发现，约 70% 的小鸭更倾向于走向与它们第一次看到的物体保持同种形状或颜色关\n系的那一对物体。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文是 the same shape or color relationship——这里 or 表示 \"形状或颜色\" 二者之\n一的关系，不是把形状和颜色 \"关联起来\"；选项 They could associate shape with\ncolor 是错读 or 的产物。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未谈及小鸭 \"反应速度\"；选项 They reacted quickly to moving objects 是利用\nmoving objects 这一原词，凭空加上 quickly 这一速度属性。\nC) 无中生有\n原文只提到不同颜色（green、blue、orange、purple）之间的对照，从未涉及\n\"colorless 无色\" 物体；选项 They preferred colored objects to colorless ones 凭空\n造出有色与无色的对比。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe same shape or color relationship as the first objects they saw ↔\nThey could tell whether the objects were the same\nmake inferences from same and different ↔\nThey could tell whether the objects were the same\n预判 \"小鸭能识别同异关系\"，They could tell whether the objects were the same 与\n之精确对应；tell whether X were the same 正是 same-different discrimination 的\n口语化表述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What was novel about the experiment in the study reported in Science?", "options": ["A) The animals used received no training.", "B) It used a number of colors and shapes.", "C) It was conducted by experimental psychologists.", "D) The ducklings were compared with other animals."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What was novel about the experiment...? 问该实验本身新颖在哪里，是对实验属性的客\n观提问，答案在原文有直接对应。\n【拆句】题干主干 What was novel about the experiment；过去分词短语 reported in Science\n后置修饰 the study，相当于 which was reported in Science。novel 在此意为 \"前所未有\n的、首次的\"，与原文 for the first time 直接呼应。\n【定位】By studying imprinting, the authors of this study have shown for the first time that an animal\ncan learn relationships between concepts without training, said Jeffrey Katz, an experimental\npsychologist at Auburn University who was not involved in the study.\nAuburn 大学的实验心理学家 Jeffrey Katz（未参与本研究）表示，通过研究印随现象，本\n研究作者首次证明动物可以在 \"无需训练\" 的情况下学会概念之间的关系。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nan animal can learn relationships between concepts without training ↔\nThe animals used received no training\nuntrained newborn ducklings ↔ The animals used received no training\n预判 \"未受训练即首次\"，The animals used received no training 与之严丝合缝；A 是\nwithout training 与 untrained 的标准动宾化改写。\nB) 答非所问\n原文 two objects ... in shape or color 提到使用了不同颜色和形状，但这只是实验材料\n的描述，不是 \"新颖之处\"——只是惯常做法；选项 It used a number of colors and\nshapes 把背景信息冒充为新颖之处。\nC) 答非所问\nexperimental psychologist 与 experimental psychologist 只是介绍研究人员身份，\n从未将 \"实验心理学家\" 当作本研究的新颖之处；选项 It was conducted by\nexperimental psychologists 把背景信息错认为答案。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 Previous studies have suggested that other animals ... can discern same\nfrom different 是用来反衬本研究新颖性的，而不是说本实验 \"把小鸭与其他动物作了比\n较\"；选项 The ducklings were compared with other animals 误把语段功能当成实验\n设计。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do we learn from Dr. Wasserman's comment on the study of animal minds at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) It is getting more and more intricate.", "B) Research methods are being updated.", "C) It is attracting more public attention.", "D) Remarkable progress is being made."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from Dr. Wasserman's comment...? 限定具体人和具体位置，问其评\n论传达的客观信息，仍是细节题，不是推断题。\n【拆句】主干 What do we learn from Dr. Wasserman's comment；on the study of animal\nminds 是介词短语限定 comment 的内容范围；at the end of the passage 是位置状语限\n定 comment 的物理位置。注意 comment on 是固定搭配，意为 \"对……的评论\"。\n【定位】We are in a revolutionary phase in terms of our ability to understand the minds of\nother animals.\n在理解其他动物心智的能力方面，我们正处于一个 \"革命性的阶段\"。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 far more of the intricacies in their world 说的是 \"动物世界的复杂性\"，不是 \"研\n究本身越来越复杂\"；选项 It is getting more and more intricate 把 intricacies 这一原\n词扎根，但用错了对象。\nB) 无中生有\nWasserman 谈的是认知层面的革命，不是方法层面的革新；原文未提 research\nmethods are being updated。\nC) 无中生有\nWasserman 谈的是学界对动物心智认知的进展，没有提及 \"公众关注\" 这一话题；选项\nIt is attracting more public attention 完全偏离。\nD) ✓ 正确\nrevolutionary phase in terms of our ability to understand the minds of other animals ↔\nRemarkable progress is being made\nanimals process and appreciate far more of the intricacies in their world than we've ever\nunderstood\n↔ Remarkable progress is being made\n预判 \"动物心智研究取得突破\"，Remarkable progress is being made 把\nrevolutionary phase 这一比喻翻译为 \"显著进展\"，是定位句的标准还原。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What has been a standard piece of dietary advice for decades?", "options": ["A) People should cultivate a healthy eating habit.", "B) People should have a well-balanced diet.", "C) People should diversify what they eat.", "D) People should limit calorie intake to avoid obesity."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What has been a standard piece of dietary advice for decades? 直接发问\"几十年\n来标准建议是什么\"，要的是文本陈述的客观事实。\n【拆句】What has been a standard piece of dietary advice for decades? 是 What 引导的特殊\n疑问句，谓语用现在完成时 has been，强调\"从过去几十年延续至今的状态\"，与 for\ndecades 时间状语呼应；无陷阱。\n【定位】The suggestion that people should aim for dietary diversity by trying to eat a variety of\nfoods has been a basic public health recommendation for decades in the United States\nand elsewhere.\n几十年来，在美国及其他地方，建议人们通过尝试多种食物来追求饮食多样化，一直是一条\n基本的公共健康建议。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\ncultivate a healthy eating habit 是合理的常识建议，但原文标准建议特指\"多样化\"而\n非泛泛的\"健康习惯\"——属于范围模糊的偷换。\nB) 偷换概念\nwell-balanced diet 在中文里和\"多样化\"语感相近，但原文用的是 dietary diversity，\n强调\"种类多\"而非\"营养均衡\"，两者不等值。\nC) ✓ 正确\naim for dietary diversity by trying to eat a variety of foods ↔ diversify what they eat\n预判要的是\"使饮食多样化\"，C 项 diversify what they eat 就是这层意思的标准同义改\n写，锁定 C。\nD) 张冠李戴\nlimit calorie intake to avoid obesity 是文章后文 obesity 话题的延伸，但题干问的\n是\"几十年来的标准建议\"，原文标准建议是 diversity 而非\"限制热量\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What did the new research by the American Heart Association find?", "options": ["A) People seeking dietary diversity tend to eat more.", "B) Big eaters are more likely to become overweight.", "C) Dietary diversity is positively related to good health.", "D) Unhealthy food makes people gain weight more easily."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did the new research by the American Heart Association find? 问\"研究发\n现了什么\"，找的是研究结论的客观陈述。\n【拆句】What did the new research by the American Heart Association find? 是 What 引导的\n一般疑问倒装，主干是 the new research found what，by 短语作后置定语；结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Researchers reviewed all the evidence published related to dietary diversity and saw a\ncorrelation between dietary diversity and a greater intake of both healthy and unhealthy\nfoods. This had implications for obesity, as researchers found a greater prevalence of obesity\namongst people with a greater dietary diversity.\n研究者回顾了所有已发表的与饮食多样化相关的证据，发现饮食多样化与摄入更多健康和不\n健康食物之间存在相关性。这对肥胖问题有重要含义，因为研究者发现，饮食多样化程度更\n高的人群中肥胖患病率也更高。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na correlation between dietary diversity and a greater intake of both healthy and unhealthy foods\n↔ People seeking dietary diversity tend to eat more\n预判是\"多样化与吃得更多相关\"，A 项 People seeking dietary diversity tend to eat\nmore 即此意，且与原文 correlation 关系无矛盾，锁定 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nBig eaters are more likely to become overweight 的主语是\"吃得多的人\"，而原文论\n述对象是\"追求多样化的人\"；\"吃得多→变胖\"是常识，但不是本研究发现的关系。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文说多样化与\"更多肥胖患病率\"相关，是负面相关；positively related to good\nhealth 却说与\"健康\"正相关——意思完全相反。\nD) 无中生有\nUnhealthy food makes people gain weight more easily 是常识，但原文研究发现的\n是\"多样化与吃得更多/肥胖率高\"相关，并非\"不健康食物本身致胖\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What could help to explain the contradiction between the new findings and the common public health recommendation?", "options": ["A) Most dietary guidelines around the world contradict one another.", "B) Conventional wisdom about diet is seldom supported by science.", "C) The methods researchers use to measure nutrition vary greatly.", "D) There is little consensus on the definition of dietary diversity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What could help to explain the contradiction 要求找原文给出的解释，答案是文本\n中明示的原因陈述。\n【拆句】What could help to explain the contradiction between the new findings and the\ncommon public health recommendation? 主干是 What could help to explain the\ncontradiction，between A and B 双介短语作 contradiction 的后置定语；结构略长但平\n铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】But this advice does not seem to be supported by science, possibly because there is\nlittle agreement about the meaning of \"dietary diversity,\" which is not clearly and\nconsistently defined.\n但这一建议似乎并未得到科学的支持，可能是因为对于\"饮食多样化\"的含义，学界几乎没有\n共识——它本身就没有清晰一致的定义。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nMost dietary guidelines around the world contradict one another 把\"指南间彼此\n矛盾\"安给原文，原文实际说的是\"新研究与指南矛盾\"，而不是\"指南之间矛盾\"。\nB) 过度推断\nConventional wisdom about diet is seldom supported by science 把原文\"这条建议\n似乎未被科学支持\"扩大为\"所有饮食常识都很少被科学支持\"，从个例跳到普遍规律。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe methods researchers use to measure nutrition vary greatly 把\"测量饮食多样\n化的方法不同\"偷换为\"测量营养的方法不同\"——nutrition ≠ dietary diversity，主体\n偏离。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthere is little agreement about the meaning of \"dietary diversity\" ↔\nThere is little consensus on the definition of dietary diversity.\n预判\"定义没共识\"，D 项 little consensus on the definition of dietary diversity 是其\n标准同义改写，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What did Dr. Rao find after 20 years of research on obesity?", "options": ["A) Dietary diversity promotes healthy body weight.", "B) Eating patterns and weight gain go hand in hand.", "C) Diversified food intake may not contribute to health.", "D) There is no clear definition of optimal eating patterns."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did Dr. Rao find after 20 years of research on obesity? 锁定具体人物的具体\n发现，是典型的人物观点细节题。\n【拆句】What did Dr. Rao find after 20 years of research on obesity? What 引导一般疑问倒\n装，after 短语作时间状语；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Dr. Rao, one of the study authors, noted that, after 20 years of experience in the field of\nobesity, he has observed that people who have a regimented lifestyle and diet tend to be\nthinner and healthier than people with a wide variety of consumption.\n研究作者之一 Rao 博士指出，凭借 20 年肥胖研究领域的经验，他观察到饮食和生活方式有\n规律的人，往往比饮食种类繁多的人更瘦、也更健康。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nDietary diversity promotes healthy body weight 说\"多样化促进健康体重\"，与原文\nRao 的观察（多样化的人不如规律饮食的人瘦）方向完全相反。\nB) 答非所问\nEating patterns and weight gain go hand in hand 谈\"饮食模式与体重增加同步\"，与\n原文 Rao 的观察焦点（多样化 vs 规律化的比较）不对位，且 Rao 句中并未直接给出这\n一结论。\nC) ✓ 正确\npeople who have a regimented lifestyle and diet tend to be thinner and healthier than people with\na wide variety of consumption\n↔ Diversified food intake may not contribute to health.\n预判是\"多样化未必有利于健康/体重\"，C 项 Diversified food intake may not\ncontribute to health 用否定形式表达同义，锁定 C。\nD) 张冠李戴\nno clear definition of optimal eating patterns 把 53 题里\"dietary diversity 没有清\n晰定义\"换成\"optimal eating patterns 没有清晰定义\"，并把这个内容安到 Rao 的发现\n头上——既偷换概念又移花接木。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the passage say about people who eat a great variety of food?", "options": ["A) They tend to consume more sweets, sugar and red meat.", "B) They don't feel they have had enough until they overeat.", "C) They don't have any problems getting sufficient nutrition.", "D) They are more likely to eat foods beneficial to their health."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about people who eat a great variety of food? 要求\n找原文对该群体的直接陈述。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about people who eat a great variety of food? What 引\n导特殊疑问句，about 介短语后接 people who... 限定性定语从句修饰 people；结构平铺\n直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Further, there is some evidence that a greater variety of food options in a single meal may\ndelay people's feeling of fullness and actually increase how much they eat.\n此外，还有一些证据表明，一餐中食物种类越多，可能会推迟人们的饱腹感，反而让他们吃\n得更多。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\ntend to consume more sweets, sugar and red meat 出自末段研究者建议的\"少吃这\n些\"，并不是\"饮食多样的人实际吃得更多\"的内容——把第四段研究者的建议张冠到第三\n段对群体的描述上。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmay delay people's feeling of fullness and actually increase how much they eat ↔\nThey don't feel they have had enough until they overeat.\n预判是\"饱腹感推迟、吃过头\"，B 项 don't feel they have had enough until they\novereat 是这一因果链的同义改写，锁定 B。\nC) 无中生有\ndon't have any problems getting sufficient nutrition 谈\"获取营养无障碍\"，原文从\n未涉及\"营养是否充足\"这个话题——属凭空捏造。\nD) 与原文相反\nmore likely to eat foods beneficial to their health 说\"更可能吃有益健康食物\"，与原\n文 Rao 的观察（多样化者更胖更不健康）方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
